    <node id="Title 11">
      <data key="d0">Title 11</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Title 11 is a comprehensive legal framework that encompasses a variety of regulations primarily focused on tree preservation, environmental protections, and land use. This title operates within a broader regulatory context and may have overlapping provisions, particularly those related to flood hazard areas in conjunction with other codes such as Title 33.

The primary content of Title 11 revolves around the governance of tree removal, grading, and permitting processes. It establishes specific standards for the preservation of trees during urban development, including density requirements and protective measures for existing trees. The regulations mandate adherence to tree requirements and outline protocols for on-site tree preservation and protection, contributing significantly to urban green space initiatives.

Moreover, Title 11 includes provisions for the preservation and protection of street trees, as well as landscaping requirements that come into effect during development activities. It mandates that permits be obtained before tree removal can occur under certain circumstances, ensuring calculated compliance with environmental considerations. Overall, Title 11 serves as a pivotal entity in promoting sustainable development practices while safeguarding urban forestry and environmental integrity.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-caa2864f9db143b33db40ca11a516e63&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9d8a7c5169d0acda3f7ceac0b42dee14&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bc2b499d5c2e57ccff7be29d1a99659e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-751caacf2242e09d6dc20cc909dc88a4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c84bdf4402c70c4d4ae69283e01b9ab4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5348591467a41c77d858f5dbd717c172&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-feff08f8742feb2b38fee34824605dc0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-99992d12911944db6172dcc6b2ae4ab5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8cfa9c8fb1cdec53d0022f9be528e80a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cc5679c2a480cf928223a40dfabf825d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b0460f02d0e013f6aa12de53db834a01&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-59602cfae2cfed1834328c9ad24adf39&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b45e62181c08d6aef102ffa4458a5226&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-495efc01559ae21a68e9da3b57998769</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1768781074</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Title 33">
      <data key="d0">Title 33</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Title 33 refers to a comprehensive set of planning and zoning regulations established in the City of Portland, addressing a wide array of land use, development standards, and associated processes. It governs various aspects including home occupations, transient lodging, and building permits for demolition, while also providing specific requirements for different zoning classifications. Among its numerous chapters and sections, Title 33 incorporates regulations pertinent to single-dwelling zones, multi-dwelling zones, commercial and mixed-use zones, and campus institutional zones.

The regulations within Title 33 also engage with environmental protections, outlining guidelines related to environmental zones, landscaping, tree preservation, and resource management. It includes provisions for community design standards and historic resource management, particularly with respect to alterations in designated historic overlay zones. The document impacts urban design through regulations on construction, landscaping, transportation demand management, and building height standards across the various plan districts, such as the Central City and Gateway districts.

Moreover, Title 33 encompasses rules for land divisions, conditional uses, and development impacts, mandating compliance with procedures for land use reviews and permits. The document delineates requirements for community planning, including standards for nonconforming situations and the management of outdoor structures. By outlining explicit standards for development, transportation, and community design, Title 33 serves as a regulatory framework that guides urban development while aiming to harmonize environmental, aesthetic, and functional needs within the community.</data>
