    <node id="City Council">
      <data key="d0">City Council</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">The City Council is the governing body responsible for various legislative and regulatory functions within the city, particularly in relation to land use, development, and urban planning. It plays a crucial role in approving appointments to the Building Code Board of Appeal and other civic entities, as well as overseeing compliance with city regulations. The City Council is empowered to adopt fines and penalties associated with enforcement of demolition regulations and must approve mitigation plans in collaboration with Metro for waste-related uses.

Additionally, the City Council is involved in the approval process for significant development proposals, which may include requiring design reviews and initiating environmental assessments related to zoning amendments. The body also holds the authority to approve exceptions to development regulations under greenway guidelines and Greenway Goal Exceptions. Its responsibilities extend to conducting final reviews of land use actions, legislative decisions, and significant updates to the Comprehensive Plan.

The City Council is also tasked with adopting area plans as part of the Comprehensive Plan, which guide development and land use policies across various neighborhoods, including specific regions such as Marquam Hill and the South Waterfront Greenway. Furthermore, it engages with recommendations from commissions and other bodies regarding legislative actions, land use, and zoning map amendments. During its review processes, the City Council receives recommendations from the Hearings Officer on amendments to the Comprehensive Plan Map and is responsible for making final decisions regarding appeals against previous review body decisions.

In summary, the City Council serves a vital function in local governance, overseeing multiple aspects of urban development, land use planning, legislative actions, and regulatory compliance while ensuring community interests are represented in decision-making processes.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f2934c450fcc75ab9c66c0de792267b0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-60990f5dab82c2b537b14b43e0a453ca&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7a7ce09240c58c0153b8be45ed992be9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-69705150e078dd993ef14bd699a3bc12&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a10cf031f788aa8206369e4c6c630d3d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-11138328735cf87d245bb64f2a57f4d9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8650a4518ea9b5abb022622589521156&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f92d57fd526433c19e4bdb08e2032fe0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a451e7e8d08a7ee4503b05d9e1fac269&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3b04cb33b3135ed3ccce916cd1c14a77&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1d42b3010c15664b1fb208cc57e4facc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f6848b70101fce6283f62065768fd727&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6638b6f4daad5d15b558bf7e5689cbbb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ac7f584fe857b5fce6394c52a4277eee&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9a90d16f519debce0d1b06def34f6932&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-467b12ebf0546021f51b7429432586d0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-53ce319f7fcb7893676edd6b489a8b48&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-aa32294cb8855f6032b144b5ecb04092&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-644cc4e15ec9d767b69917b94f282c47&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d8ee84d998824c1408d6153086fc99b0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c9c1ff44e4a06a65a47b8c351d06d59b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-256d1ee4f0b33311aca7f325efe86843&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ec40727e0d8bf940e01c40131ec7d220&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dbde9b26d1da0b6d201fb666deeea1af&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dc65f441d5b262a6e985063f42142907&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0a414888338825a53493117bb9837d0c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b60dcf59d4eb9520c7bf8e9968ecf967&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-94f04c4574ac8473fb645f2bd083c638&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-29ada1c6efdf6913783f9994bb070ef8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-599508fe86fb4e6ee4dd67e02ddb8b50&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f4e7feaddec557941c697fec7b2e2763&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2d28f6febcf4de65239d0203f2197382&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cebb99c668ae3666c0d99752b2945f3c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dc255f6ca548e358b62f9a60708754b0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f9daedca8ce38c66f76f89f5342070ce&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7fce67d5a707ef7b953fc44e3ab5449b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-88614ee4358558cf8b16aa8620bf9f9b</data>
