    <node id="Department of Forestry (DOF)">
      <data key="d0">Department of Forestry (DOF)</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">The Department of Forestry is the State of Oregon's agency involved in assessments related to wildfire hazards.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-43230c8c64a3e12675c36b28cd80b9f7</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1768779848</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Wildfire Hazard Zone">
      <data key="d0">Wildfire Hazard Zone</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The Wildfire Hazard Zone refers to areas designated by the Chief of Portland Fire &amp; Rescue that meet specific criteria for wildfire risks.&lt;SEP&gt;The Wildfire Hazard Zone is a designated area that indicates the potential for wildfire behavior and impacts within the City.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-43230c8c64a3e12675c36b28cd80b9f7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e74fbd35235f431d27b1fedb2323fc8b</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1768779848</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Wildfire Hazard Zone Map">
      <data key="d0">Wildfire Hazard Zone Map</data>
      <data key="d1">artifact</data>
      <data key="d2">The Wildfire Hazard Zone Map is a document detailing areas classified as wildfire hazards, adopted by ordinance.&lt;SEP&gt;The Wildfire Hazard Zone map identifies areas that are at risk of wildfires and dictates building compliance in those areas.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-43230c8c64a3e12675c36b28cd80b9f7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7e8c1c7b39585a7a7e3106344983f67e</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1768779848</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Hazard Factors">
      <data key="d0">Hazard Factors</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Hazard factors include topography, natural vegetative fuels, and their distribution, which influence wildfire risks in designated areas.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-43230c8c64a3e12675c36b28cd80b9f7</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1768779848</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Wildfire Hazard Zone Criteria">
      <data key="d0">Wildfire Hazard Zone Criteria</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Wildfire Hazard Zone Criteria denote the standards and classifications established to determine areas at risk of wildfires within the City of Portland.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-43230c8c64a3e12675c36b28cd80b9f7</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1768779848</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Ordinance No. 177433">
      <data key="d0">Ordinance No. 177433</data>
      <data key="d1">artifact</data>
      <data key="d2">Ordinance No. 177433 is the legal document that includes the Wildfire Hazard Zone Map and the criteria for its establishment and amendments.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-43230c8c64a3e12675c36b28cd80b9f7</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1768779848</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="ASCE 7-16">
      <data key="d0">ASCE 7-16</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">ASCE 7-16 is a standard that must be followed for designing various structural elements, as referenced in the text.&lt;SEP&gt;ASCE 7-16 is a standard that outlines design criteria for structures, including requirements for soil loads and retaining walls.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-88b0a262fe914a0c7fcb75c7815be4a6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f8d41d5cef81888f4a8f2657cd2edf9d</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1768779848</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Retaining Walls">
      <data key="d0">Retaining Walls</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Retaining walls are structures designed to retain soil and must be engineered for various pressures and conditions, including hydrostatic pressure.&lt;SEP&gt;Retaining wall regulations specify construction standards for stability and aesthetics.&lt;SEP&gt;Retaining walls serve to mitigate negative effects on aesthetics and safety, with height and setback regulations outlined in the standards.&lt;SEP&gt;Retaining walls are structures designed to support soil laterally so it can be retained at different levels on two sides, which have specific regulations in urban planning.&lt;SEP&gt;Retaining Walls are structures that hold back soil and are measured from the higher side to determine their height.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-88b0a262fe914a0c7fcb75c7815be4a6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e58a4abdc96df0823cedabefcc94943d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-40b451e83bd236d5610bd2be4f5e2e51&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6feb38e3d4faebf120b7cf1e9165848c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-495efc01559ae21a68e9da3b57998769</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1768781055</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Soils Engineer">
      <data key="d0">Soils Engineer</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">The Soils Engineer provides evaluations and recommendations regarding soil stability related to retaining walls.&lt;SEP&gt;The Soils Engineer is responsible for evaluating soil suitability and determining competent material for construction foundations.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-88b0a262fe914a0c7fcb75c7815be4a6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f8d41d5cef81888f4a8f2657cd2edf9d</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1768779848</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Figure No. 2">
      <data key="d0">Figure No. 2</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">Figure No. 2 provides visual standards for the design and placement of retaining walls in relation to property lines and setbacks.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-88b0a262fe914a0c7fcb75c7815be4a6</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1768779848</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Table No. 24.70-C">
      <data key="d0">Table No. 24.70-C</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">Table No. 24.70-C includes numerical design standards and setback requirements for grading and retaining walls.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-88b0a262fe914a0c7fcb75c7815be4a6</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1768779848</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Terrace">
      <data key="d0">Terrace</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">A terrace is a level step constructed on a graded slope mainly for drainage and maintenance purposes.&lt;SEP&gt;Terraces are horizontal surfaces designed on slopes to control drainage and must adhere to specified dimensions and intervals.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-88b0a262fe914a0c7fcb75c7815be4a6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-706782d1588e72967037b624ff644c38</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1768779848</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Culverts">
      <data key="d0">Culverts</data>
      <data key="d1">artifact</data>
      <data key="d2">Culverts are structures that allow water to flow under roads or embankments and must be designed to maintain the hydraulic characteristics of streams.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-88b0a262fe914a0c7fcb75c7815be4a6</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1768779848</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Dynamic Seismic Lateral Earth Pressure">
      <data key="d0">Dynamic Seismic Lateral Earth Pressure</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Dynamic seismic lateral earth pressure refers to the additional pressure exerted on retaining walls during seismic events, requiring special design considerations.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-88b0a262fe914a0c7fcb75c7815be4a6</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1768779848</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Monobe-Okabe Method">
      <data key="d0">Monobe-Okabe Method</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">The Monobe-Okabe method is a calculation technique used to determine active dynamic seismic lateral earth pressure on retaining walls.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-88b0a262fe914a0c7fcb75c7815be4a6</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1768779848</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Load Combinations of ASCE 7-16">
      <data key="d0">Load Combinations of ASCE 7-16</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Load combinations outlined in ASCE 7-16 are specific criteria for various structural load considerations during design and do not apply to some retaining wall requirements.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-88b0a262fe914a0c7fcb75c7815be4a6</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1768779848</data>
      <data key="d6" />
