    <node id="Oregon Mechanical Specialty Code">
      <data key="d0">Oregon Mechanical Specialty Code</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The Oregon Mechanical Specialty Code includes specifications for mechanical systems within buildings, as adopted by reference in Title 24.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-4f1c1a120b8ba477f5a407d0777a44e3</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1768779849</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="City Engineer">
      <data key="d0">City Engineer</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">The City Engineer is an official within the City of Portland tasked with a variety of responsibilities centered around the management and approval of public infrastructure and street-related designs. This role involves overseeing the approval of routes for moving buildings, ensuring compliance with street regulations, and approving plans for commercial buildings and off-street parking areas.

In addition to these responsibilities, the City Engineer is responsible for approving designs and improvements in the public right-of-way, which includes nonstandard improvements that require review before the historic resource application. The City Engineer also plays a crucial role in establishing landscaping standards in scenic corridor setbacks and adjacent rights-of-way.

Collaboration with other city officials, such as the City Forester, is part of the City Engineer's duties, especially in the approval of street tree plans for public streets. The position demands that the City Engineer ensures all street designs meet safety and operational standards, addressing any concerns that may arise during the planning stages. This may include requiring Transportation Impact Studies for land divisions to mitigate safety or operational issues effectively.

Moreover, the City Engineer has jurisdiction over street vacations and developmental engineering issues, overseeing any necessary mitigation projects and public service reviews, thus maintaining a comprehensive oversight of urban planning and development related to public streets and right-of-ways.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-407245345ef716e5e0d52e90aa6e4851&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8e6462811b852b5096dfd03e84ed0979&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bbc54ab4dbbc617d4c752a69733a5f32&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f92d57fd526433c19e4bdb08e2032fe0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e1ee11d492136a3cb6116df2011b3bcd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-992f79974821c7d84a0abe4219c3b2b6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-877a823bd216070dbe9fd06f8befc552&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-083abb85def98a799fa4d4f0c3d8ab61&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-22acd36bdbe56e46354926a0904b7463&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cd9adaf9df20165e7a81ed8dcfe84163&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fc617f6f881f3f98f97136cfa84f2ea2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-23bdb6b78c43cbe3cee1a2bacbb57a5f</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1768781030</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="City Traffic Engineer">
      <data key="d0">City Traffic Engineer</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">The City Traffic Engineer reviews plans for off-street parking areas before permits are issued.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-407245345ef716e5e0d52e90aa6e4851</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1768779849</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Fire Marshal">
      <data key="d0">Fire Marshal</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">The Fire Marshal reviews plans for certain dwelling repairs and remodels before approval by the City Administrator.&lt;SEP&gt;The Fire Marshal is a role responsible for overseeing fire safety standards at helicopter landing facilities.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-407245345ef716e5e0d52e90aa6e4851&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-21fff4b96769197d29b5065fb50894e2</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1768781121</data>
