    <node id="Site">
      <data key="d0">Site</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">A site is defined as a lot or parcel of land where grading is performed or permitted, under the same ownership.&lt;SEP&gt;A site is a designated area where market activities, construction, and public gatherings are permitted as per zoning regulations.&lt;SEP&gt;Site refers to lots or tracts proposed for division or reconfiguration, and the ownership relating to those lots for development purposes.&lt;SEP&gt;A site is a specific area of land that is subject to development or planning under municipal regulations.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-706782d1588e72967037b624ff644c38&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cc1a993d28187168df19c579664230fc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-58d46c43c9570caf791239d0c34502c3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1ad3df252c10f7d3d9869375a3dd70b5</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1768781054</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Slope">
      <data key="d0">Slope</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">A slope is characterized as an inclined ground surface, defined by a ratio of horizontal to vertical distance.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-706782d1588e72967037b624ff644c38</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1768779851</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Soil">
      <data key="d0">Soil</data>
      <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
      <data key="d2">Soil consists of naturally occurring deposits that overlie bedrock and serve as the foundation for structures.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-706782d1588e72967037b624ff644c38</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1768779851</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Soil Engineering">
      <data key="d0">Soil Engineering</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Soil (geotechnical) engineering applies soil mechanics principles for evaluating and designing civil works using earth materials.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-706782d1588e72967037b624ff644c38</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1768779851</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Grading Permit">
      <data key="d0">Grading Permit</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">A grading permit is an official authorization required for performing grading work on a site, subject to regulations.&lt;SEP&gt;The Grading Permit is an authorization required for grading operations, which may be modified based on weather-related delays.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-706782d1588e72967037b624ff644c38&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f8d41d5cef81888f4a8f2657cd2edf9d</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1768779851</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Plans and Specifications">
      <data key="d0">Plans and Specifications</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Plans and specifications are detailed documents that must be submitted to indicate the nature and extent of proposed grading work.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-706782d1588e72967037b624ff644c38</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1768779851</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Erosion Control Measures">
      <data key="d0">Erosion Control Measures</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Erosion control measures involve practices used to prevent soil loss during and after grading operations.&lt;SEP&gt;Erosion control measures refer to strategies and structures used to prevent soil erosion, especially in development contexts.&lt;SEP&gt;Erosion Control Measures are temporary actions taken to prevent soil erosion until permanent solutions are established.&lt;SEP&gt;Erosion Control Measures are techniques designed to prevent soil erosion and protect the environment during construction and development activities.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-706782d1588e72967037b624ff644c38&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-148b4481af37fbad429e170e0527ee60&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cfece86ae05751fbebb6324df767074f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cc5679c2a480cf928223a40dfabf825d</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1768781078</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Civil Engineering Report">
      <data key="d0">Civil Engineering Report</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">A civil engineering report includes hydrological calculations and storm drainage capacity details required for grading permits.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-706782d1588e72967037b624ff644c38</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1768779851</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Soil Engineering Report">
      <data key="d0">Soil Engineering Report</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">A soil engineering report provides data on soil characteristics and recommendations for development based on soil analysis.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-706782d1588e72967037b624ff644c38</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1768779851</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Flood Hazard Area">
      <data key="d0">Flood Hazard Area</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The Flood Hazard Area refers to geographic regions identified as susceptible to flooding. These areas are formally recognized due to the risks posed by flooding, prompting the implementation of specific regulations and restrictions aimed at minimizing potential impacts. In these designated zones, development is heavily regulated to prevent or mitigate risks associated with flooding, which can include substantial damage to property and safety hazards for residents.

Flood Hazard Areas are integral to zoning regulations, impacting construction practices and development approvals. Developers are typically required to adhere to strict guidelines intended to protect both properties and the environment. These guidelines may include limitations on construction types and practices, environmental regulations regarding tree removal and planting, and criteria that govern lot development and replat approval.

In many instances, Flood Hazard Areas are defined based on historical data indicating periodic or regular flooding, and they necessitate the implementation of special mitigation measures to safeguard against flooding events. The objective is to maintain safety and reduce risk in communities vulnerable to flooding, ensuring that the development within these regions does not exacerbate the inherent dangers while promoting responsible land use. 

Additionally, a subcategory known as the Combined Flood Hazard Area may include further restrictions and a comprehensive approach to risk management, highlighting the importance of careful planning and compliance with environmental standards in these sensitive areas.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-1db63c93213cb06b155d2b22811e90c9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2a06ad2f76c555340d2a3febc2894de5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bc2b499d5c2e57ccff7be29d1a99659e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-134e99fe1ed81e0af91dacf0ec909533&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a64960cc4adf91b7f6ea0964bf7f6dbd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1f39dddebb1be94c3bd4cfa1ac35ccac&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bdee8c33d0d52a7455e70087137ca492&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b33894864a61e3795f8a49e185ee7bcb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-899c4b9a2ac9d3d9713b5cef39a863b0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cfece86ae05751fbebb6324df767074f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-95645a65574ce270cd08fff187fbb27d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-83e6483ee1a0cf1415621eb7ccf6224b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cd8537f80e77714b5b0188914023a593&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-70d37b0db2824c732a3a9a02ff910d85</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1768781040</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Flood Insurance Rate Map">
      <data key="d0">Flood Insurance Rate Map</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Flood Insurance Rate Map (FIRM) is the official map that delineates areas of special flood hazards.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-1db63c93213cb06b155d2b22811e90c9</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1768779851</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Flood">
      <data key="d0">Flood</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Flood describes a temporary condition of inundation from the overflow of water or rapid accumulation of surface waters.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-1db63c93213cb06b155d2b22811e90c9</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1768779851</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Flood Protection Elevation">
      <data key="d0">Flood Protection Elevation</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Flood Protection Elevation is the design flood elevation plus a freeboard allowance to mitigate flood risks.&lt;SEP&gt;Flood protection elevation is the required height to which structures must be built to prevent flood damage, applicable to all new construction within flood hazard areas.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-1db63c93213cb06b155d2b22811e90c9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2a06ad2f76c555340d2a3febc2894de5</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1768779851</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Floodplain">
      <data key="d0">Floodplain</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Floodplain refers to the channel of a watercourse and adjacent land areas at risk of inundation during a design flood.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-1db63c93213cb06b155d2b22811e90c9</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1768779851</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Floodplain Administrator">
      <data key="d0">Floodplain Administrator</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">The Floodplain Administrator oversees the administration and enforcement of flood-related regulations and permits.&lt;SEP&gt;Floodplain Administrator is the city official responsible for administering and enforcing flood-related regulations.&lt;SEP&gt;The Floodplain Administrator oversees compliance with flood-related regulations and documentation.&lt;SEP&gt;The Floodplain Administrator is a governing authority responsible for approving agreements related to flood risk management and ensuring compliance with flood protection standards.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-1db63c93213cb06b155d2b22811e90c9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2a06ad2f76c555340d2a3febc2894de5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-134e99fe1ed81e0af91dacf0ec909533&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-21ee09234912e67470de67393ca93ace</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1768779851</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Floodproofing">
      <data key="d0">Floodproofing</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Floodproofing includes structural and non-structural measures taken to prevent flood damage to properties.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-1db63c93213cb06b155d2b22811e90c9</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1768779851</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Flood Fringe Area">
      <data key="d0">Flood Fringe Area</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Flood Fringe Area is the land outside the floodway that is susceptible to flooding during a base flood event.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-1db63c93213cb06b155d2b22811e90c9</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1768779851</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Freeboard">
      <data key="d0">Freeboard</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Freeboard is an additional height above the design flood elevation to account for factors that may increase flood heights.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-1db63c93213cb06b155d2b22811e90c9</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1768779851</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Functionally-Dependent Use">
      <data key="d0">Functionally-Dependent Use</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Functionally-Dependent Use refers to activities or structures that need proximity to water to serve their purpose.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-1db63c93213cb06b155d2b22811e90c9</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1768779851</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="High Hazard Area">
      <data key="d0">High Hazard Area</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">High Hazard Area refers to regions at high flood risk, defined as areas inundated by floods with a 10% chance of occurrence in a year.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-1db63c93213cb06b155d2b22811e90c9</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1768779851</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Hydrologically Connected">
      <data key="d0">Hydrologically Connected</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Hydrologically Connected describes the interaction between groundwater and surface water impacting the overall water supply.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-1db63c93213cb06b155d2b22811e90c9</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1768779851</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Lowest Floor">
      <data key="d0">Lowest Floor</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Lowest Floor refers to the lowest habitable area within a structure, not including certain enclosures.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-1db63c93213cb06b155d2b22811e90c9</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1768779851</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Design Flood">
      <data key="d0">Design Flood</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Design flood is the greater of the base flood and the February 1996 Flood Inundation Area, representing critical flood conditions for planning.&lt;SEP&gt;Design Flood refers to the flood level for which the floodplain management measures are designed.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-1db63c93213cb06b155d2b22811e90c9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-134e99fe1ed81e0af91dacf0ec909533</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1768779851</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Base Flood">
      <data key="d0">Base Flood</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The base flood, also known as the 100-year flood, signifies a flood event with a 1% chance of occurrence in any year.&lt;SEP&gt;Base Flood is defined as the flood with a 1% chance of being equaled or exceeded in any given year.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-1db63c93213cb06b155d2b22811e90c9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-134e99fe1ed81e0af91dacf0ec909533</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1768779851</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Floodplain Management">
      <data key="d0">Floodplain Management</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Floodplain Management encompasses the policies and practices aimed at reducing flood risks and impacts on communities.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-1db63c93213cb06b155d2b22811e90c9</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1768779851</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="County Recorder's Office">
      <data key="d0">County Recorder's Office</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">The County Recorder's Office is the institution where maintenance agreements and access easements must be recorded.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-616337655b3a110b38e3cef90183ec87</data>
