    <node id="Fuel Model 1">
      <data key="d0">Fuel Model 1</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Fuel Model 1 describes a grass type where very little shrub or timber is present, and fires move rapidly through cured grass and associated material.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e7e9c0aa1580e6a1ab39c745a0f5e202</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1768779852</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Fuel Model 2">
      <data key="d0">Fuel Model 2</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Fuel Model 2 pertains to open shrub lands and pine stands that cover one-third to two-thirds of the area, primarily consisting of herbaceous fuels.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e7e9c0aa1580e6a1ab39c745a0f5e202</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1768779852</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Fuel Model 3">
      <data key="d0">Fuel Model 3</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Fuel Model 3 includes beach grasses, prairie grasses, and wild or cultivated grains that have not been harvested, characterized by high rates of fire spread.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e7e9c0aa1580e6a1ab39c745a0f5e202</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1768779852</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Fuel Model 4">
      <data key="d0">Fuel Model 4</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Fuel Model 4 describes stands of mature shrubs known for their flammability, with high-intensity burns and rapid spread.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e7e9c0aa1580e6a1ab39c745a0f5e202</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1768779852</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Fuel Model 5">
      <data key="d0">Fuel Model 5</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Fuel Model 5 refers to young shrubs with little dead material, where fires are not very intense and are carried in surface fuels.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e7e9c0aa1580e6a1ab39c745a0f5e202</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1768779852</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Fuel Model 6">
      <data key="d0">Fuel Model 6</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Fuel Model 6 represents older shrubs that have moderate flammability, with fires dropping to the ground at low wind speeds.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e7e9c0aa1580e6a1ab39c745a0f5e202</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1768779852</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Fuel Model 8">
      <data key="d0">Fuel Model 8</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Fuel Model 8 pertains to areas of timber with little undergrowth and small amounts of litter buildup, burning only under extreme weather conditions.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e7e9c0aa1580e6a1ab39c745a0f5e202</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1768779852</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Fuel Model 9">
      <data key="d0">Fuel Model 9</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Fuel Model 9 refers to timber areas with more surface litter than Fuel Model 8, where fire spreads can be enhanced by rolling leaves.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e7e9c0aa1580e6a1ab39c745a0f5e202</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1768779852</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Fuel Model 10">
      <data key="d0">Fuel Model 10</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Fuel Model 10 deals with timber that has heavy buildups of ground litter, making fires difficult to control due to the fuel extent.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e7e9c0aa1580e6a1ab39c745a0f5e202</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1768779852</data>
