    <node id="Common Open Areas">
      <data key="d0">Common Open Areas</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Common Open Areas are shared spaces in residential developments that provide recreational and aesthetic benefits to residents.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f4254e6eb792730b58dedb0301cbb63d</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1768780999</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Residential Infill Options">
      <data key="d0">Residential Infill Options</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Residential infill options allow for additional housing within established neighborhoods.&lt;SEP&gt;Residential Infill Options facilitate the development of diverse housing types while maintaining the character of neighborhoods.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f4254e6eb792730b58dedb0301cbb63d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-40b451e83bd236d5610bd2be4f5e2e51</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1768780999</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Triplexes and Fourplexes">
      <data key="d0">Triplexes and Fourplexes</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Triplexes and Fourplexes are types of residential structures that consist of three and four units respectively, governed by specific zoning standards.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f4254e6eb792730b58dedb0301cbb63d</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1768780999</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Table 110-7">
      <data key="d0">Table 110-7</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">Table 110-7 provides required minimum lot areas for triplexes and fourplexes under the zoning regulations outlined in Title 33.&lt;SEP&gt;Table 110-7 outlines the minimum lot area requirements for triplexes and fourplexes in different zoning categories.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f4254e6eb792730b58dedb0301cbb63d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a1daea73587f156d45df1e0850a2216e</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1768780999</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Setback Requirements">
      <data key="d0">Setback Requirements</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Setback Requirements dictate the minimum distances that structures must be from property lines or public streets in zoning regulations.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f4254e6eb792730b58dedb0301cbb63d</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1768780999</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Residential Housing Types">
      <data key="d0">Residential Housing Types</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Residential Housing Types encompass various forms of housing, including single-family homes, duplexes, triplexes, and fourplexes, permitted in specific zoning areas.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f4254e6eb792730b58dedb0301cbb63d</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1768780999</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Nonconforming Development Assessment">
      <data key="d0">Nonconforming Development Assessment</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The Nonconforming Development Assessment evaluates developments that do not conform to regulations and determines compliance requirements for applicants.&lt;SEP&gt;The Nonconforming Development Assessment identifies development that does not meet the current standards and is required for permit applications.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3e735d7bf40b332127efe9da20dd2f85&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-608046d7256ca4fab03fcfe5e6c5d66b</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1768780999</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Compliance Period">
      <data key="d0">Compliance Period</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The Compliance Period is a timeframe allocated for addressing nonconforming aspects of a development, with durations varying based on square footage.&lt;SEP&gt;The compliance period is the timeframe within which all required improvements must bring a site into conformance with standards after a building permit is issued.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3e735d7bf40b332127efe9da20dd2f85&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-608046d7256ca4fab03fcfe5e6c5d66b</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1768780999</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Option 1">
      <data key="d0">Option 1</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Option 1 is a permanent built-in irrigation system equipped with an automatic controller to water plants.&lt;SEP&gt;Option 1 is a choice available to applicants for addressing nonconforming development, though specific details are not explicitly provided.&lt;SEP&gt;Option 1 refers to a choice for making required improvements during alterations, which limits costs to 10 percent of the proposed alterations' value.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3e735d7bf40b332127efe9da20dd2f85&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-608046d7256ca4fab03fcfe5e6c5d66b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-82ef9b400f3f8eaf84f03bdff42a7221</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1768780999</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Option 2">
      <data key="d0">Option 2</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Option 2 refers to a certified irrigation system designed by a licensed landscape architect as part of a landscape plan.&lt;SEP&gt;Option 2 is an alternative choice for applicants that involves a separate Nonconforming Development Assessment and compliance period for new nonconforming developments.&lt;SEP&gt;Option 2 allows required improvements to be made over several years, subject to a specified compliance period.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3e735d7bf40b332127efe9da20dd2f85&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-608046d7256ca4fab03fcfe5e6c5d66b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-82ef9b400f3f8eaf84f03bdff42a7221</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1768780999</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Nonconforming Exterior Development">
      <data key="d0">Nonconforming Exterior Development</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Nonconforming Exterior Development refers to exterior developments that do not meet compliance regulations, losing rights if unused for specified periods.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3e735d7bf40b332127efe9da20dd2f85</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1768780999</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Parking Spaces">
      <data key="d0">Parking Spaces</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Parking Spaces relate to the number of vehicle parking provisions, which can be retained or redesigned in nonconforming sites under specified conditions.&lt;SEP&gt;Parking Spaces refers to designated areas where vehicles can be parked, which are regulated under local zoning laws and standards.&lt;SEP&gt;Parking spaces refer to designated spots for parking vehicles, with regulations differing by zone.&lt;SEP&gt;Parking spaces that have regulated increases or decreases based on set percentages and total amounts.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3e735d7bf40b332127efe9da20dd2f85&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f8966d01e665f9cb17191db9bb5912d2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-555b254650c4e6bcde7e85291a2480b7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-deccf49edc8456f9e632a30209a8e62a</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1768780999</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Chapter 33">
      <data key="d0">Chapter 33</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Chapter 33 contains the regulations for planning and zoning related to manufactured parks and housing in Oregon.&lt;SEP&gt;Chapter 33 contains guidelines and regulations related to planning and zoning, providing a comprehensive framework for nonconforming situations and assessments.&lt;SEP&gt;Chapter 33 encompasses regulations related to planning and zoning, including permitting processes and environmental zones.&lt;SEP&gt;Chapter 33 refers to a section of regulations that governs planning and zoning in Portland, particularly regarding parking.&lt;SEP&gt;Chapter 33 contains regulations regarding planning and zoning, including development standards for urban institutional campuses.&lt;SEP&gt;Chapter 33 pertains to planning and zoning regulations, detailing various plan districts and their specific standards and guidelines.&lt;SEP&gt;Chapter 33 contains planning and zoning regulations as they apply to various districts including Powell Boulevard and Rocky Butte.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3e735d7bf40b332127efe9da20dd2f85&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fc573198faf9c1b51eb551532d91476d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-393312325b82278116f59418428cf4e7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-13c2d06abd310f2993f5dbfa38449c5b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0d9e8d9a2a7e63e60180b8dfe9e9acda&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-78755af7dcf0cbd31bb8a59f246d601c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-760f8bc033fa383332ea7e6ae26dff29</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1768780999</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
