    <node id="Historic Landmarks Commission">
      <data key="d0">Historic Landmarks Commission</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">The Historic Landmarks Commission is a governing body tasked with advising on and overseeing the preservation of historic resources, particularly within the City of Portland. It plays a critical role in preserving the city’s architectural heritage and ensuring that any alterations to historic features adhere to design guidelines established for Historic and Conservation Districts.

This commission comprises members with relevant expertise, which allows it to provide informed leadership on preservation initiatives. The Historic Landmarks Commission is involved in reviewing historic landmarks and conservation landmarks, with its powers and duties assigned by the City Council. The commission is responsible for making recommendations regarding preservation efforts and alterations to historic sites, emphasizing the importance of maintaining the integrity of these resources.

Furthermore, the Historic Landmarks Commission reviews proposals for historic landmarks and forwards its advisory comments to the appropriate review body, ensuring that all initiatives align with established preservation standards. It also plays a role in legislative processes concerning the designation or removal of historic districts, reflecting its influence on land use and planning related to historical preservation. Overall, the commission is essential for fostering a respectful and informed approach to the management of Portland's historical assets.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-69705150e078dd993ef14bd699a3bc12&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ea6bbc03052089bd83b779b573dbdac6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8650a4518ea9b5abb022622589521156&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3b04cb33b3135ed3ccce916cd1c14a77&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1d42b3010c15664b1fb208cc57e4facc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f6848b70101fce6283f62065768fd727&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ac7f584fe857b5fce6394c52a4277eee&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4ca2fc1b73b6e1ae4e82cbaf205ce719&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dc65f441d5b262a6e985063f42142907&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b60dcf59d4eb9520c7bf8e9968ecf967&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3bde6fb397c7366841124f779d183b6c</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1768781009</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Planning Commission">
      <data key="d0">Planning Commission</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">The Planning Commission is a governmental body tasked with a variety of responsibilities related to land use and urban planning. It is primarily responsible for reviewing planning proposals and making recommendations on long-range goals, policies, and programs for land use and planning. The Commission plays a crucial role in the initiation of Pleasant Valley Resource Reviews, which are linked to development regulations, and it also has the authority to initiate amendments to the Comprehensive Plan Map.

In addition to its review responsibilities, the Planning Commission provides advice on urban planning matters, including historic district designations and conservation efforts. It serves as a review body that makes decisions on land use actions and offers recommendations for legislative land use reviews to the City Council. Furthermore, the Planning Commission is involved in ensuring compliance with planning regulations and can initiate text amendments concerning zoning and land use.

The Commission is required to hold joint public hearings related to legislative matters, particularly those that involve design standards. It also reviews recommendations made by the Design Commission and ensures that the planning process is aligned with community goals and regulatory standards. Overall, the Planning Commission plays a vital role in shaping the urban environment and guiding future development within its jurisdiction.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-69705150e078dd993ef14bd699a3bc12&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a10cf031f788aa8206369e4c6c630d3d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8650a4518ea9b5abb022622589521156&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3b04cb33b3135ed3ccce916cd1c14a77&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1d42b3010c15664b1fb208cc57e4facc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f6848b70101fce6283f62065768fd727&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9a90d16f519debce0d1b06def34f6932&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ec40727e0d8bf940e01c40131ec7d220&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dc65f441d5b262a6e985063f42142907&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-599508fe86fb4e6ee4dd67e02ddb8b50&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cebb99c668ae3666c0d99752b2945f3c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-88614ee4358558cf8b16aa8620bf9f9b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-500735b30bf92bffd639e639d7f98132</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1768781009</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Development Standards for Institutions">
      <data key="d0">Development Standards for Institutions</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Development Standards for Institutions encompasses the regulations governing building height and other related standards for institutional uses.&lt;SEP&gt;Development standards for institutions outline the specific regulations applicable to institutional uses in multi-dwelling zones.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-199bdfd5f31802f14bb3bd7efff3a92e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-63c21c957d68787ba4fea52897f3a3b8</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
