    <node id="Rights-of-Way">
      <data key="d0">Rights-of-Way</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Rights-of-Way refer to legal rights that allow passage through specific areas or property for transportation or utility purposes.&lt;SEP&gt;Rights-of-way refer to legal rights to pass through property, often applied to transportation and utility corridors.&lt;SEP&gt;Rights-of-Way are designated spaces for transportation infrastructure, including streets and sidewalks, ensuring safe access.&lt;SEP&gt;Rights-of-way are designated paths for public or private access and their ownership is defined through specific standards and regulations.&lt;SEP&gt;Rights-of-Way refer to legal rights allowing passage over a specific route or path, particularly relevant in urban planning and public infrastructure.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-bbc54ab4dbbc617d4c752a69733a5f32&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9e1459a3e9df8bdd44292209ab034270&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-992f79974821c7d84a0abe4219c3b2b6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dd5e24f7c98b2b609a3ed6656b608420&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-22acd36bdbe56e46354926a0904b7463</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1768781017</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Private Streets">
      <data key="d0">Private Streets</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Private Streets are access routes designed for limited use, serving a small number of properties where public access is not feasible.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-bbc54ab4dbbc617d4c752a69733a5f32</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1768781017</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Private Alleys">
      <data key="d0">Private Alleys</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Private Alleys are designated for limited vehicle access and support parking consolidation while allowing pedestrian and bicycle use.&lt;SEP&gt;Private Alleys refer to pathways that provide vehicular or pedestrian access within residential or commercial developments, often paralleling private streets.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-bbc54ab4dbbc617d4c752a69733a5f32&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-992f79974821c7d84a0abe4219c3b2b6</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1768781017</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="R2.5 Zone">
      <data key="d0">R2.5 Zone</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The R2.5 Zone is a zoning classification primarily utilized for residential development, mandating a minimum of two dwelling units on lots that are at least 5,000 square feet in size. This zoning category specifies that the minimum lot area for each dwelling must be 1,500 square feet, thus allowing for a higher density of residential structures compared to the R10 Zone.

In addition to these density requirements, the R2.5 Zone encompasses various regulations concerning development standards, including floor area ratios and building heights. It is particularly relevant for configurations like attached houses and cottage clusters, which must adhere to minimum area and density specifications. Properties within the R2.5 Zone are also permitted to establish one accessory dwelling unit on compliant lots, which can include attached or manufactured homes.

The R2.5 Zone functions similarly to the R5 zoning classification but has distinct regulations tailored to property configurations, including the potential for flag lots. Within its jurisdiction in cities like Portland, the R2.5 Zone allows for specific residential structures and infill development options, making it a critical category in the planning and development of residential areas. Moreover, this zoning designation may also factor into calculations regarding development rights for residential units, further enhancing its importance in urban planning.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5af8d828cbb38f1d71e4c9300bd4b4cc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b65a020b68966aec020e3367f56ac854&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a1daea73587f156d45df1e0850a2216e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f3d3dc1f275b21f3a0c1622695839a94&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c60b235ce896bb310db6bf647fb59f27&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-85949d3f78909228a152d02b0d54f684&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e889d237f01a5093e629880a38d5a9d6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-95645a65574ce270cd08fff187fbb27d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3c2d80fd8407d1e9806e03445c7d0a7c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-83e6483ee1a0cf1415621eb7ccf6224b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8b8e38ccecc8aee043d4f983d468a91b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a7c158baba6b66776b2fa39c55edd02f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cfe92a3f46b3bfc4baa1f040d200709a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7fee5077b9b5bae2a7dec413478071a4</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1768781018</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Turnarounds">
      <data key="d0">Turnarounds</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Turnarounds are designed junctions on dead-end streets allowing vehicles to safely reverse direction without backing up for long distances.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-bbc54ab4dbbc617d4c752a69733a5f32</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1768781018</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Commercial/Mixed Use Zone">
      <data key="d0">Commercial/Mixed Use Zone</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The Commercial/Mixed Use Zone encompasses land-designated areas for various types of development and usage, including specific regulations related to their utilization.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7cb0a8fdf55836bf22ae8a840cfbe18d</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1768781018</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Aviation and Surface Passenger Terminals">
      <data key="d0">Aviation and Surface Passenger Terminals</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Aviation and Surface Passenger Terminals are designated as uses within the Commercial/Mixed Use Zone, subject to specific regulatory conditions.&lt;SEP&gt;Aviation and surface passenger terminals are transportation hubs with designated parking requirements based on net building area.&lt;SEP&gt;Aviation and Surface Passenger Terminals are facilities permitted in the Airport Subdistrict under specific zoning regulations.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7cb0a8fdf55836bf22ae8a840cfbe18d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5cc02978e3319b39c1038d83f233d71f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b80bff0fbd16752d5db014bfe9946815</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1768781018</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Chapter 33.110">
      <data key="d0">Chapter 33.110</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Chapter 33.110 is a specific section within Title 33, which encompasses regulations relating to Planning and Zoning, primarily focusing on Single-Dwelling Zones. This chapter delineates comprehensive zoning regulations that govern various aspects of land use, design requirements, and building standards specifically tailored for single-dwelling areas.

Within Chapter 33.110, detailed guidelines are provided concerning setback requirements, building height, and coverage standards, which are crucial for ensuring appropriate spatial relationships between structures. The chapter also stipulates regulations regarding landscaped areas, the construction and regulation of detached and connected accessory structures, and standards pertinent to fencing and retaining walls in these zones. 

Additionally, the chapter outlines stipulations for cottage clusters and articulates planning requirements that are applicable to both single-dwelling and multi-dwelling zones. By encompassing a wide range of criteria, Chapter 33.110 serves as a foundational element in the planning and zoning framework, ensuring that developments within single-dwelling zones adhere to established standards and promote orderly land use and development.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5af8d828cbb38f1d71e4c9300bd4b4cc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-65928458722d6696f7fafa7beda4c38d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b65a020b68966aec020e3367f56ac854&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c2e452b9ec8a9af12b429462d8859d26&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-24a0dc5a40ce1a7a9401f80aac49c163&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-619bfdcc25aa9b9b415e2291e2bb9041&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c7a3faa53abe19fba4ba510e5addfc1e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3c2d80fd8407d1e9806e03445c7d0a7c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8b8e38ccecc8aee043d4f983d468a91b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-72db2074515977339664a4c6fb188af1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9a48525044c3c1bf75799ef0954937b7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-495efc01559ae21a68e9da3b57998769&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-13fbd22ccb86e669dc7d330d54f31def</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1768781018</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Mining">
      <data key="d0">Mining</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Mining is an activity related to other categories and allowed conditionally in Single-Dwelling Zones.&lt;SEP&gt;Mining encompasses the process of extracting minerals and resources from the earth.&lt;SEP&gt;Mining is categorized as a prohibited use within the Commercial/Mixed Use Zone, providing land use restrictions.&lt;SEP&gt;Mining refers to sites involved in the extraction of minerals, which come with specific parking requirements.&lt;SEP&gt;Mining refers to the extraction of minerals or other geological materials, with parking indicated as subject to conditions.&lt;SEP&gt;Mining encompasses the extraction of mineral or aggregate resources from the ground for off-site use.&lt;SEP&gt;Mining represents land use related to the extraction of natural resources from the earth.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7cb0a8fdf55836bf22ae8a840cfbe18d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d984c1f88be9e0bd0ee4f857384d9046&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-16b2323b48f41f87137658c4c38c98ae&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c7a3faa53abe19fba4ba510e5addfc1e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-34de9e34b3c0011d322a8520a1091e13&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c2d22ee5e42bbb3db37d28f3801d3229&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4551ce1e7653dda2331ab8ba0712bbf7</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1768781018</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Planning and Zoning">
      <data key="d0">Planning and Zoning</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Planning and Zoning refers to the comprehensive framework of rules and standards that govern land usage and development within specific jurisdictions. This regulatory system encompasses a wide range of elements, including zoning regulations, building requirements, development standards, and guidelines that dictate how land can be utilized and developed, particularly in urban areas.

The concept of Planning and Zoning includes regulations targeting multi-dwelling zones, conditional land uses, and building designs, ensuring that developments align with community objectives and standards. It also covers specifics such as height limits, alterations to existing structures, transportation considerations, tree management, and the designation of historical districts and resources. 

In the context of urban planning, Planning and Zoning serves to organize and direct land usage in a manner that promotes sustainable development while addressing environmental concerns. Local municipalities often implement these regulations to manage orderly growth, maintaining community character while accommodating future development.

Moreover, the Planning and Zoning framework involves processes for decision-making regarding land use, including public hearings and appeals, which are integral for transparency and community engagement. Within this realm, there are specific provisions that relate to greenway zones and drive-through facilities, illustrating the breadth of implications Planning and Zoning has on daily urban life and development practices.

Overall, Planning and Zoning embodies the legal foundation that channels both land use and community development efforts, striving for a balanced approach between development and the preservation of community standards and environmental integrity.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-1eff4d5f9f592ba50c4796217ad50f4a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fb1ab98a275a3e96b292afb310b8237d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e58a4abdc96df0823cedabefcc94943d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a966c4eaabe25aca0a1be25bd188299e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9e1459a3e9df8bdd44292209ab034270&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5d220a61d92d1c965d2882acdcd160d7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6e17537bd24336b2dd65681e37f4d9aa&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bd57b33ee4fb90b8e15b4036d8b2f9e3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-05ab6bd54c89f86d004823d37a33255e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8b56454c04ddb2d2d976f1d6f6ebbd60&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b3f313ee8f50d5cdb880e71ed5eab739&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-abc8929a3f5f26daf160f30332a927b6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cc6e3473a810a12fa40e385deedea49f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8f9fa0abfcd845e14321262101dfa625&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3ec46b8cc5650199d033fa9573b44034&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6b0271e8b27cedc9b0b135f529688a98&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-207bdd149614a77764e1c1161b9089e5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-78724ed583edac4d2f2940a886eda8af&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2faa24df5ddab0e7d0172ca67c9e0da6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-616a6925a0ab31d5e3e594ef74a2e21d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ff254476c2742dcdf5c26a1256fd36e8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a9400bf4e5a50bd8a9d73707eb530e30&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-55b0bd3b13a0c05d569d6126ad180bab&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d735bdaad55969341cee3cca65a09caf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-aa32294cb8855f6032b144b5ecb04092&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0ab589ad2a81c7f7964b172f2ba2949a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-58aaae1598e79c7b72066b698da3a9b7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-33f6d86139e9f8ebbadc462769659fed&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6798d89accc1ff81b347ffdd52d0e6c7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c05d0565960f8c21b2582374b4856a38&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-185d37e595bd772f81423b42b3193eca&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e52cd6f75c0ef15f81f473a7fd946bbc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-028c3c84ee58774048f304cfbc47c40a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-37fe28a6c33ecbcbe2396156e4ee10e6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-49385b6cdb2ddda6bb336746708e24c5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2001ba277158737c9b888248e680d7ee&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-594964a698a9063f34a66127a99f84d2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c7643c0f4d3b8e865b2421a8cf1882d9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-da8cd0a693bdee1928f1f0600d5193c3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-924adce58a3a3a8622d21ee99005b5a7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-67111441a3c9d2875734e7bedb42d3af&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-44cf0420ca13ed6c3eed49130f77fb31&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7fce67d5a707ef7b953fc44e3ab5449b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5786b7f8c1ac1620534c8b4098445f90&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7b7c5b32a510f170e1363780c7ccaa64</data>
