    <node id="Self-Contained Pedestrian Connection">
      <data key="d0">Self-Contained Pedestrian Connection</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">A self-contained pedestrian connection is designed solely for pedestrians and bicycles, enhancing accessibility in urban environments.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-fb1ab98a275a3e96b292afb310b8237d</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1768781022</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Cottage Clusters">
      <data key="d0">Cottage Clusters</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Cottage Clusters are a type of residential development that has specific zoning exemptions and standards.&lt;SEP&gt;Cottage clusters are groups of homes that are subject to specific zoning standards and regulations in the RM1 zone.&lt;SEP&gt;Cottage clusters are groups of small houses that are exempt from certain design standards regarding entrances.&lt;SEP&gt;Cottage clusters refer to a type of development consisting of small, clustered homes, often surrounded by communal spaces.&lt;SEP&gt;Cottage Clusters represent a specific type of residential design intended for small homes that may share common space within the zoning regulations.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-fb1ab98a275a3e96b292afb310b8237d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-59644a82d0120b0f13d2a9d402862b9b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2c54aa01b8a84d0bf5d8975d9c8cc8b4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b2a185269200e9d231c5a69d32eb78c8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-606354f765e28b95d29b753fc9fe43fd</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1768781022</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="RM2 Zone">
      <data key="d0">RM2 Zone</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The RM2 Zone is a medium-density multifamily residential zoning classification primarily found in urban centers and transit-served corridors. This zoning designation permits the construction of buildings ranging from three to four stories, accommodating growth and development in areas with significant infrastructure support.

Within the RM2 Zone framework, there are specific regulations and standards that govern various aspects of property development. One notable regulation stipulates a minimum density requirement of 1 unit per 2,000 square feet for residential lots with front lot lines on shared courts or common greens. The zone also incorporates unique building setback norms, which dictate how far structures must be from property boundaries, to ensure adequate spacing and design consistency in residential properties.

Furthermore, the RM2 Zone allows for the potential transfer of density from RMP-zoned sites, enhancing flexibility in development opportunities. This classification also includes guidelines for outdoor areas in residential developments, ensuring that open spaces are adequately planned and utilized. Additionally, it possesses regulations on surface parking and limitations on building area, contributing to a balanced development approach.

The RM2 Zone designation is part of the broader Lombard Street Plan District's planning and zoning regulations, emphasizing its role in guiding medium-density residential development while maintaining specific standards for the local environment. Overall, the RM2 Zone serves as a comprehensive framework aimed at facilitating structured growth within designated urban areas.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a9bca75f94d2f6ca963676dd23e65338&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eded02aea3f115ba99e1a14dd25ad585&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-912c86bea48b8b39caf895ea538d23c4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3b0bbb62dc601b56a2d362f9a82f2ea6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9ac355b146a97e19880b3790c5ffe83a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-35d4506f9183e501d03cc84da18500af&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-13fbd22ccb86e669dc7d330d54f31def&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8cfa9c8fb1cdec53d0022f9be528e80a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f3901520644b000ca953e813d65ccc92&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0d9e8d9a2a7e63e60180b8dfe9e9acda&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-de298aa875613309b192e347dc83983f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b1267f51ecc30ce027ed225a2c473621&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e57a10d5dc00da1681573c18ad3bbe22</data>
