    <node id="Floor Area Ratio">
      <data key="d0">Floor Area Ratio</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The Floor Area Ratio (FAR) is a critical regulatory measure that establishes the relationship between the total floor area of a building and the land area it occupies. FAR is particularly significant in urban planning and zoning regulations, as it determines the maximum allowable building size on a given site, influencing the overall density and usage of land within designated zones.

FAR is applied across different types of properties, including attached houses, and is used to regulate the intensity of land use. It is an essential factor in the Commercial/Mixed Use Zone, where it defines the bulk and intensity of development. By calculating the ratio of the total building floor area to the parcel area, zoning authorities can set limits on building heights and sizes, thus controlling the density of development in various areas.

In addition to being a tool for regulating development bulk, Floor Area Ratio can also influence decision-making in construction by determining bonus height allowances. Different exemptions may apply within various contexts; for instance, cottage clusters might not be subject to standard FAR calculations. Overall, Floor Area Ratio plays a pivotal role in shaping urban environments by guiding sustainable development and balancing land-use patterns.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7cb0a8fdf55836bf22ae8a840cfbe18d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-40b451e83bd236d5610bd2be4f5e2e51&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-85949d3f78909228a152d02b0d54f684&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e691024552ab10508d9e2848f48d8859&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fe493d8ddd3157de8f252cc78bfde3db&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3c2d80fd8407d1e9806e03445c7d0a7c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0ce4a0b57a87c710f0ef76d5570894bf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f2ae74afee809cbeca89eeeb5025a2bf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b2003f93c2ff07e93de1875ffe1520e0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4177bfbc917fc82fda498c4d1f0e571f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5b1548cc5d56ac0b8d2ab93d83d89c89</data>
