    <node id="Application">
      <data key="d0">Application</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">The application is the formal request submitted by the applicant for a land use review, which must comply with specific requirements.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-14945c9dafbb0b5a4864c97b10515585&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3f69badbdc0a01b4a234db008584a915&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7c35c570b66abd053c37dd3812df90d3</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1768781034</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Public Hearing">
      <data key="d0">Public Hearing</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">The Public Hearing is an event where the reconsideration process is discussed, requiring notice to the operator and public.&lt;SEP&gt;The public hearing is a scheduled event where appeal hearings are held to address decisions made by the local government.&lt;SEP&gt;The Public Hearing is a scheduled meeting where stakeholders can present information and views regarding land use decisions.&lt;SEP&gt;A public hearing is scheduled to review applications for land use changes and provides an opportunity for public comment.&lt;SEP&gt;Public Hearings are conducted to gather testimony and address decision criteria related to land use applications.&lt;SEP&gt;Public Hearing is a formal event where community members provide input and comments on proposed legislative actions affecting land use.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-14945c9dafbb0b5a4864c97b10515585&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4d6359633c178583bad809c59ed226cf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-644cc4e15ec9d767b69917b94f282c47&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dc65f441d5b262a6e985063f42142907&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0a414888338825a53493117bb9837d0c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b60dcf59d4eb9520c7bf8e9968ecf967&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4a43129b93ff8ead9df972bce81ecbb2</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1768781034</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Final Decision">
      <data key="d0">Final Decision</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The final decision is the conclusive ruling made by the Hearings Officer after considering the appeal.&lt;SEP&gt;Final Decision refers to the outcome that follows the completion of a quasi-judicial review process.&lt;SEP&gt;The final decision of the review body is effective on the day the notice of decision is mailed.&lt;SEP&gt;The Final Decision refers to the official approval granted after processing a land use application, which must be recorded to permit usage.&lt;SEP&gt;The Final Decision is the concluding authorization pertaining to land use applications and developments.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-14945c9dafbb0b5a4864c97b10515585&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-07e86c208ee49379750e080df5cf9722&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0a414888338825a53493117bb9837d0c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-594964a698a9063f34a66127a99f84d2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3bde6fb397c7366841124f779d183b6c</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1768781034</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Type I Procedure">
      <data key="d0">Type I Procedure</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">The Type I Procedure is a process used for resource enhancement activities within the Pleasant Valley Resource Review framework.&lt;SEP&gt;Type I procedure is an administrative process involving public notice without a hearing, used for certain land use reviews.&lt;SEP&gt;Type I Procedure is an administrative procedure for making decisions on minor design and historic resource cases.&lt;SEP&gt;Type I Procedure is one of the procedural classifications for land use reviews, indicating the simplest level of review.&lt;SEP&gt;The Type I Procedure is the simplest review process applicable to specific minor development changes and alterations.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-14945c9dafbb0b5a4864c97b10515585&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a10cf031f788aa8206369e4c6c630d3d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-07e86c208ee49379750e080df5cf9722&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-809349c43acd176bb535a05d03c6c5c3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3bde6fb397c7366841124f779d183b6c</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1768781034</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Chapter 33.420">
      <data key="d0">Chapter 33.420</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Chapter 33.420 is a specific section within Title 33 that outlines planning and zoning regulations, particularly focusing on design overlay zones. This chapter defines additional design standards and development requirements that must be adhered to within these overlay areas. It specifies the necessary construction standards and requirements that govern the development processes in relation to design overlay zones.

This section serves as a comprehensive guideline for city planners, developers, and other stakeholders involved in urban development, as it encompasses various aspects of zoning and planning relevant to the unique characteristics of design overlay zones. Chapter 33.420 establishes both the intent and the regulatory framework for ensuring that developments within these zones meet established aesthetic and functional criteria, thereby contributing to the overall vision for urban development outlined in the broader planning legislation.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b0081003d43e7b772e5a8d3dfae39548&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5c96a1e5b1eb94f3a586e5986daa73c7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-207bdd149614a77764e1c1161b9089e5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2faa24df5ddab0e7d0172ca67c9e0da6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c1643cca9962c7d0ac7fc6f08d56ed54&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0c749708919a67aa6d2facb52ce88c3d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-34a0bcc2ef40b300fb0774b97f4e1e7f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ecbcd7b658791e5d0719e505f91ad147</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1768781034</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Property Owners">
      <data key="d0">Property Owners</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Property owners are individuals whose properties are within a specified distance from the site and are notified during the application process.&lt;SEP&gt;Property Owners are individuals who own property within a specified distance of the site and are notified regarding design advice requests.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-14945c9dafbb0b5a4864c97b10515585&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-699f7379e9519e29f504f42bb94daaf0</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1768781035</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Recognized Organizations">
      <data key="d0">Recognized Organizations</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">Recognized organizations refer to groups acknowledged within the PIR Master Plan process, which must be notified of relevant amendments.&lt;SEP&gt;Recognized Organizations are groups within the proximity of the application site that receive notifications and may provide input on the application.&lt;SEP&gt;Recognized organizations are groups that are notified regarding appeals and decisions in land use reviews based on their participation in the process.&lt;SEP&gt;Recognized organizations are groups that respond to appeal notices and are contacted during the review process for land use applications.&lt;SEP&gt;Recognized Organizations represent groups whose boundaries include the site and whose contact information is required in the notices.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-14945c9dafbb0b5a4864c97b10515585&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3f69badbdc0a01b4a234db008584a915&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a86ce100d88532ab8ca2f1d785c9a636&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f63396adc71a2e9185adb3bc7b8db5ad&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6c623ec1ba96e21d348f6cf747ed9156</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1768781035</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Transportation System Plan">
      <data key="d0">Transportation System Plan</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The Transportation System Plan provides a framework for managing transportation infrastructure and services in conjunction with urban development in Central City.&lt;SEP&gt;The Transportation System Plan is designed to manage the supply of off-street parking and enhance urban mobility.&lt;SEP&gt;The Transportation System Plan outlines targets related to mode share and auto ownership that developments must aim to achieve.&lt;SEP&gt;The Transportation System Plan outlines transportation infrastructure and service requirements over a 20-year period.&lt;SEP&gt;The Transportation System Plan outlines regulations and classifications for streets and traffic management.&lt;SEP&gt;The Transportation System Plan outlines classifications and standards for various streets, including service types.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-84efb40ac49907b061aeb00bb2d08948&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-61e934d288b532969001ac5c602e3192&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-185d37e595bd772f81423b42b3193eca&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e2c50fc0ff9e807e9d3489aba2b4c9f0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dbee049dd9c661ea5a90ad39db328689&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dd435149dc3e05a98f074fcc3dd3ac12</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1768781035</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Partial Street">
      <data key="d0">Partial Street</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">A Partial Street refers to segments of a street that may be located on different sites and are created when a street is completed in stages.&lt;SEP&gt;A partial street consists of segments from a street that may be completed in stages across different sites.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-84efb40ac49907b061aeb00bb2d08948&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-61e934d288b532969001ac5c602e3192</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1768781035</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Shared Court">
      <data key="d0">Shared Court</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">A shared court is designed to allow shared access for vehicles, pedestrians, and bicycles on the same surface, possibly functioning as a community yard.&lt;SEP&gt;Shared Court refers to a common area intended for use by multiple parties.&lt;SEP&gt;A Shared Court is a designed street area accommodating vehicles, pedestrians, and bicycles, emphasizing shared circulation space.&lt;SEP&gt;A shared court is a street designed to accommodate access for vehicles, pedestrians, and bicycles within the same space.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-84efb40ac49907b061aeb00bb2d08948&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-61e934d288b532969001ac5c602e3192&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cc4ad1e18166e18f3ffc6869780387e3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-94d2f2ba51a73a23de500a4e54b5f682</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1768781035</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Street">
      <data key="d0">Street</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Street is a public roadway that provides access for vehicles and pedestrians.&lt;SEP&gt;A Street serves as a right-of-way for vehicles, pedestrians, or bicycles, allowing access to adjacent properties.&lt;SEP&gt;A street is a developed area allowing vehicular and pedestrian access, categorized into various types.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-84efb40ac49907b061aeb00bb2d08948&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-61e934d288b532969001ac5c602e3192&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-94d2f2ba51a73a23de500a4e54b5f682</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1768781035</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Chapter 33.475">
      <data key="d0">Chapter 33.475</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Chapter 33.475 is a designated section of Title 33 that establishes comprehensive standards concerning planning, zoning, and environmental considerations specifically related to development projects. This chapter encompasses a broad range of regulations that aim to govern various aspects of land use and development within the context of the River Overlay Zones, particularly in the Portland area.

The section addresses critical factors such as standards for residential docks, lighting, and bird-safe glazing, ensuring that development aligns with environmental management practices. It details the rules for river setbacks, affecting how properties adjacent to waterways are developed and utilized. Additionally, Chapter 33.475 encompasses regulations concerning the removal or remediation of hazardous substances, reinforcing the commitment to environmental protection and safety.

Furthermore, this chapter outlines regulations pertaining to property line adjustments within the River Overlay Zones, providing clear guidelines for compliance and modification of existing standards. The comprehensive nature of Chapter 33.475 highlights its integral role in regulating development in sensitive areas adjacent to rivers, balancing urban growth with ecological integrity and community standards.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-235ba1708a9c48c75daf3faeec3e194d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2f92d42feaabfd545cc35264c76fbed0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-60b9ffdb955ab3944250655ae5423afa&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-873d10749dbc15173a36fece7c83683d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-659ec42acd09493cb815e59e2193d7bd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f5a5816615430d9b588c4adaf4aa3131&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d65c927885cd8918e2dccfdeeb204f68&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e88e3e2ec290b90a147566b3c2e07270&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5e28ba6f352cf461311b1a12b892a5ff&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7623c76eb73dcf7a97d644a7c57616d7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8304e315438268768f83d85e05fcf463</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1768781035</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Through Street">
      <data key="d0">Through Street</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Through Street is a type of street that allows for passage through an area without closure.&lt;SEP&gt;A Through Street connects to other streets at both ends, facilitating uninterrupted traffic flow.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-84efb40ac49907b061aeb00bb2d08948&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-94d2f2ba51a73a23de500a4e54b5f682</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1768781035</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Structure">
      <data key="d0">Structure</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">A structure in this context refers to buildings that contain dwelling units and must comply with relevant development standards.&lt;SEP&gt;Structure encompasses any built object on the ground, including buildings, decks, and fences, excluding paved areas.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-84efb40ac49907b061aeb00bb2d08948&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-182c8d04df8eca72ac077fcd7a5564f3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-74ebc7af6a19551e094bffa65ccb3a60</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1768781035</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Environmental Zones">
      <data key="d0">Environmental Zones</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Environmental Zones are designated areas established to regulate land use, construction practices, and vegetation management, primarily to protect natural features, habitats, and resources. These zones ensure responsible urban planning, benefiting both the public and the environment by promoting conservation and sustainable practices. 

Specific regulations in Environmental Zones cover a variety of environmental protection measures including development standards aimed at minimizing impacts on natural resources. Such areas are governed by local regulations that dictate standards for land use, environmental preservation, and management of vegetation to help maintain functional ecological values. 

Furthermore, Environmental Zones often require compliance with particular planning and zoning regulations, which may include precise guidelines for building and vegetation management. This regulatory framework is critical to preserving ecosystems and preventing adverse environmental impacts from development activities. 

In certain jurisdictions, such as areas within the Columbia South Shore plan district, Environmental Zones serve specific regulatory roles concerning environmental protection. These zones may also contain stipulations on tree preservation and other vital natural resources to ensure comprehensive management of the ecological landscape. 

Overall, Environmental Zones play a vital role in integrating environmental considerations into urban planning and development, thereby fostering a balance between growth and ecological conservation.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-1dfee1abb6388294ddd10d180ea3f102&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a64960cc4adf91b7f6ea0964bf7f6dbd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-00a7652f0b0b380d8dc6c5c8262a6ea8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-20addac2bd7bf67d0c780bffff45e581&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-04cb5d072ac6b2a3e906facf4052c5aa&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a079f6fb5c8d245b04e7b9c62fbfa8f6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-899c4b9a2ac9d3d9713b5cef39a863b0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-393312325b82278116f59418428cf4e7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-19d39e794ab5c802105ac492ea170724&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-16b463643e19fbf1a7684090d0b63adb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9a90d16f519debce0d1b06def34f6932&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-34a0bcc2ef40b300fb0774b97f4e1e7f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-546f125167252089bdfedd0eae022ba2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-285f052019746f5b49b63ae041c898aa&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3059e173ef884a58e8cd223998e6158d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d11d225e5566d1ff994f2025b1378ec7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-70d37b0db2824c732a3a9a02ff910d85&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-55b0bd3b13a0c05d569d6126ad180bab&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8df3a6e155b7b2dbe62a2cf5f8c6d3b3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cc3a467f6b0a5b8fd0489088a17ca2d3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3a187157e908115a4998c100f65fb8a1</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1768781036</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Supermarket">
      <data key="d0">Supermarket</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Supermarket is a large retail store that sells groceries and other goods.&lt;SEP&gt;A Supermarket is a retail establishment over 20,000 square feet selling various food and household items.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-84efb40ac49907b061aeb00bb2d08948&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-94d2f2ba51a73a23de500a4e54b5f682</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1768781036</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Surface Parking">
      <data key="d0">Surface Parking</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Surface Parking refers to parking lots that are built on ground-level land as opposed to structured parking facilities.&lt;SEP&gt;Surface Parking refers to ground-level parking spaces available for vehicles.&lt;SEP&gt;Surface Parking designates areas for parking without any building area above or below the parking location.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-84efb40ac49907b061aeb00bb2d08948&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f8966d01e665f9cb17191db9bb5912d2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-94d2f2ba51a73a23de500a4e54b5f682</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1768781036</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Historic Landmark">
      <data key="d0">Historic Landmark</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">A Historic Landmark is a site or structure that is identified for its historical significance and designated under specific zoning regulations. This designation serves to highlight the site's value, which may include archaeological, architectural, cultural, or historical merit. Historic Landmarks often feature distinct zoning allowances that affect building areas and permissible uses in their vicinity.

These landmarks are not only recognized for their historical importance but also come with preservation status and standards. Such regulations may dictate design qualities for new constructions nearby, ensuring that any alterations or developments complement the historical integrity of the landmark itself. Historic Landmarks often require special consideration before any modifications, including a thorough review process for demolitions or significant changes, reflecting their importance.

Further, a Historic Landmark may qualify for certain preservation incentives, underscoring the benefit of maintaining such historically valuable sites. It is important to note that these designated properties are generally not eligible for floor area transfer and are subject to defined levels of protection against removal or alteration. The designation may also imply that the Historic Landmark is listed on the National Register of Historic Places, marking its recognized significance at a broader scale.

Overall, the Historic Landmark designation ensures that sites of considerable historical value receive recognition, protection, and mindful consideration in any planning or development activities.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b0081003d43e7b772e5a8d3dfae39548&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0a971b09f4393ef1136ed16b956179c6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f92d57fd526433c19e4bdb08e2032fe0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d31ffd348227ba062bb811bffdbe39bb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6aa735bcf86b8023079cfc5ea6f84da6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-53ce319f7fcb7893676edd6b489a8b48&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-059f078c9e0b5ea778bd163f9c511685&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8034fc51f3b39a89498e8a31263eac35&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-86420cc97941914b0b1ab8dfd5d58107&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4ab68c6faa3af9af6f5cd876312e30c7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-721ec91bd394c742ab66be71cc2391f1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-753992cd5e3f8a1085d378bbba8d5aab&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fca333850d886eb6696137e8d84c9908&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9157cebcd51ea7cec9c5bd83c0f3df47&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5a6a80015da77a2175c0409b020ea13e</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1768781036</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Temporary Disturbance Area">
      <data key="d0">Temporary Disturbance Area</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The Temporary Disturbance Area is a regulated space around a building that allows for certain types of planting and restoration activities.&lt;SEP&gt;Temporary Disturbance Area refers to zones where vegetation removal and replanting are regulated to prevent erosion and manage resource impacts.&lt;SEP&gt;The Temporary Disturbance Area includes specific site portions affected during construction that will not be permanently occupied.&lt;SEP&gt;Temporary disturbance area includes sections of a site disturbed for proposed development, which will not be permanently occupied by structures or improvements.&lt;SEP&gt;Temporary Disturbance Area refers to an area that may be disturbed temporarily for various development activities.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-84efb40ac49907b061aeb00bb2d08948&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a079f6fb5c8d245b04e7b9c62fbfa8f6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d8b0b9956e9369e20a5f76498419272b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-142263ca6fb69f507db833e40c26eab3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8263d3660b494322f6b2d93ee1245f2d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-86420cc97941914b0b1ab8dfd5d58107</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1768781036</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Through Lot">
      <data key="d0">Through Lot</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">A through lot is a lot that has frontages on two streets, where lot frontages do not intersect.&lt;SEP&gt;A Through Lot is defined under specific lot-related definitions in zoning regulations.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-84efb40ac49907b061aeb00bb2d08948&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0ab589ad2a81c7f7964b172f2ba2949a</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1768781036</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Topping">
      <data key="d0">Topping</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Topping is an extreme crown reduction method that is prohibited for required trees to maintain their natural form.&lt;SEP&gt;Topping is an inappropriate tree pruning practice that reduces height without considering health or integrity.&lt;SEP&gt;Topping is an inappropriate pruning practice used to reduce tree height by cutting to a predetermined crown limit without regard for tree health or structural integrity.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-84efb40ac49907b061aeb00bb2d08948&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5b7a46ce2a41072cf8f503e930128f86&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-82ef9b400f3f8eaf84f03bdff42a7221</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1768781036</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Tower">
      <data key="d0">Tower</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The tower represents a structure utilized for transmission purposes, requiring adherence to various height and visual impact regulations.&lt;SEP&gt;A Tower is a tall structure designed to elevate Radio Frequency Transmission Facilities above ground level.&lt;SEP&gt;A tower is a tall structure intended to elevate a Radio Frequency Transmission Facility high above the ground, typically over 20 feet tall.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-84efb40ac49907b061aeb00bb2d08948&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5b7a46ce2a41072cf8f503e930128f86&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6798d89accc1ff81b347ffdd52d0e6c7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1e0d3aa49a8c3edf6ae58238049a8d3b</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1768781036</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="L1 Standard">
      <data key="d0">L1 Standard</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The L1 Standard is a comprehensive landscaping requirement that outlines specific criteria for landscaped areas situated between a building's setback and the street lot line. This standard ensures that landscaping and screening meet ecological guidelines as detailed in Chapter 33.248, particularly focusing on urban zones and new developments.

The L1 Standard stipulates that landscaping requirements must be adhered to in building setbacks, other landscaped areas, and outdoor spaces within manufactured dwelling parks. It incorporates essential guidelines for the planting and maintenance of vegetation, including trees and ground cover, thereby enhancing the aesthetic and ecological value of these spaces.

Furthermore, the L1 Standard establishes minimum benchmarks for soil depth and other landscaping elements for compliance in various development projects. By requiring adherence to these guidelines, the L1 Standard aims to foster sustainable and visually appealing environments in urban settings.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-05299df3decd7e8061aff48bb1ba2029&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eded02aea3f115ba99e1a14dd25ad585&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-083abb85def98a799fa4d4f0c3d8ab61&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c2e596f70f1bcab1802ff190bf7807a1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8cfa9c8fb1cdec53d0022f9be528e80a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-160a32f5ce16b3d3271378c2f5cf5dac&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2d2c06f5978724095d6914de0f870d8e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3404eafc9757fc05febd370a1ac85737&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-59602cfae2cfed1834328c9ad24adf39</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1768781036</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Zoning Chapter 33.910">
      <data key="d0">Zoning Chapter 33.910</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Zoning Chapter 33.910 provides specific definitions pertinent to zoning and land use within municipalities.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-84efb40ac49907b061aeb00bb2d08948</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1768781036</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="York Plan District">
      <data key="d0">York Plan District</data>
      <data key="d1">location</data>
      <data key="d2">The York Plan District refers to a designated area in Portland that is subject to specific planning and land use regulations, as detailed in Title 33 and Chapter 33.597. This district is integral to managing urban development and includes special zoning provisions tailored for the York area. The regulations are designed to promote development that benefits the public, with a particular emphasis on affordable housing and employment opportunities.

The York Plan District encompasses a mixed-use neighborhood plan that facilitates high-density employment, residential, and commercial development, with the provisions set to take effect on June 1, 2025. To maintain an organized urban environment, the district also imposes regulations regarding floor area ratios and height limits. Overall, the York Plan District plays a crucial role in Portland’s efforts to regulate land use, ensuring it meets the city's developmental and community objectives.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-05299df3decd7e8061aff48bb1ba2029&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e32e03486b757e08e3d165c5c60acde4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e58a4abdc96df0823cedabefcc94943d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f57fb6953e9769254bb5d2a323550c3f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a5ce54762dd350a2baa6b3563a59fff3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-861d2a39c6243daf2d9eb704cb764a04&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-33d0f030f285a86371b78f7278eb5921&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5adc4cb7aae3d0001c977968b8799a6f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7792ce9cadeead80cdcf7764db7feb59&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e691024552ab10508d9e2848f48d8859&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8275cc3c3a913fa46276d690bac66384&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dd5e24f7c98b2b609a3ed6656b608420</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1768781036</data>
      <data key="d6" />
