    <node id="Subdistrict B">
      <data key="d0">Subdistrict B</data>
      <data key="d1">location</data>
      <data key="d2">Subdistrict B is a defined area within multiple planning and zoning districts, including the Cascade Station/Portland International Center plan district, the Guild’s Lake Industrial Sanctuary Plan District, the Lombard Street Plan District, and the West Portland Multicultural Plan District. As the second area in the Cascade Station/Portland International Center plan district, Subdistrict B is positioned to foster a variety of employment opportunities.

Characterized by larger office spaces, Subdistrict B also includes essential facilities such as daycare centers and colleges, all governed by distinct building regulations. It has a comprehensive set of allowed and prohibited uses, ensuring that developments align with the specific plan goals of the area. Furthermore, there are explicit development and design standards enforced within Subdistrict B, requiring compliance from all projects undertaken in its borders.

The area also has specific regulations regarding maximum parking requirements, managing the parking needs of businesses and residents effectively. Additionally, Subdistrict B participates in the Guild's Lake Industrial Sanctuary Plan District with regulations aimed at minimizing industrial conflicts. Certain types of commercial developments, including drive-through facilities, may be permitted under strict conditions.

Moreover, Subdistrict B imposes design standards on commercial parking and has specific regulations governing residential uses within its scope. It emphasizes requirements for affordable commercial space as part of the planning regulations, ensuring that developments remain accessible. Overall, Subdistrict B plays a critical role in the local economy and community planning efforts.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e038f4d3687c0baca987d663a82852cc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5d95b223ce551df4ebded5324da87138&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a4754016f12749d254a4dac403332ce8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8a373581b2a74183e9eed060dd888030&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a49a157902a76856c95e4e3a050a0ef7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c815f0219b3fa08de12163f428266e23&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0d9e8d9a2a7e63e60180b8dfe9e9acda&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7f5eb17d84fb94bd8bf31df2d43873e1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-30226dafede9cadeb254efbc901b204f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d532a006f3aa47075d5f58cb0d7da4e0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d0147b437f8af7c9b75d571f1f8e2392</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1768781047</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Covenant">
      <data key="d0">Covenant</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">A Covenant is a legally binding agreement that involves property owners and the City, often outlining conditions related to property developments and transfers. Covenants are essential in numerous contexts, including the preservation of trees, transfer of development rights, and zoning compliance. Property owners must execute these agreements to ensure adherence to specific legal and regulatory obligations.

Typically, a Covenant includes stipulations regarding density transfer between properties, maintenance and operation of open space features, and compliance with zoning regulations. For example, a Covenant can mandate that owners maintain certain conditions for the lifetime of a building, such as ensuring the continued operation of Daycare or Community Service uses. Failure to adhere to these requirements may result in enforcement actions by the City.

In addition, Covenants often reflect reduced development capacities during property transfer processes, thereby acknowledging the limitations imposed by zoning and regulatory frameworks. They can serve various functions, such as ensuring proper management of floor area ratios and protecting nonresidential use requirements. Overall, Covenants play a critical role in defining and regulating the obligations of property owners concerning urban planning and community development.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-86af9727766ff2222007c054fe1ec5f0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-903ef5f8da50cf2a59819dcb0eda03b1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c04f82ed495a847d38cb7549bbc8ce4f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-567df924f86fd66f127e58c0497a37e2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5adc4cb7aae3d0001c977968b8799a6f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-47f51b4fbd4dac0a144b2d3f573a4dd9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c815f0219b3fa08de12163f428266e23&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7d36b64a92b4e219eb8b81cf9934efff&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-77e32332dad5644a1592ba93791f21ff&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-133e0d785413e64759688100094b7c2e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8e1b5dababc725aeec628685254c591a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b8fd3cd43b3e7770531d9235f454d5b9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b9e2c7926ceca17c4d0d7614d1c8acec&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b2d0e3bbe363516aa161846014338967&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f3994dbc2c6f464ef189000d5ce6654c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-753992cd5e3f8a1085d378bbba8d5aab&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-afbed3d0d9f1ce8f7b4b9d7df85935cb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4177bfbc917fc82fda498c4d1f0e571f</data>
