    <node id="Ord. No. 178657">
      <data key="d0">Ord. No. 178657</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">Ord. No. 178657 is an ordinance that became effective on September 3, 2004. It serves as a legislative order that addresses various regulatory aspects within a specific jurisdiction. One significant aspect of this ordinance is its role in amending zoning regulations, thereby contributing to the overall development regulations.

Furthermore, Ord. No. 178657 is specifically related to regulations outlined in the Columbia South Shore Plan District, indicating its relevance to local planning frameworks. In addition to zoning amendments, the ordinance also encompasses regulations concerning tree preservation plans, highlighting a focus on environmental considerations within urban planning.

Notably, there was an amendment to the regulations associated with Ord. No. 178657 that was effective as of April 22, 2006, suggesting that the ordinance has evolved and adapted to changing regulatory needs over time. This reflects its ongoing significance within the local development landscape.

Overall, Ord. No. 178657 is a comprehensive legal framework designed to enhance zoning and planning practices, with a particular emphasis on environmental preservation and community development.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0db595deea447329ffb950dc6a8bf8b3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bcdb0ed86328221b3f3b4bc1f84ed339&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-155b41dabaadf0c9869e8d0f00d94360&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-93d39d3e5dc5ac990b049b748fa4e96e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2f2e828d0ef40701ea87d62c0b01bd2f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cd8537f80e77714b5b0188914023a593&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fd51482e13f3b991071675a0ea850c45&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-815bfa0ca1f2854481b5b5b608fdc5d0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dbde9b26d1da0b6d201fb666deeea1af&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c7f9cce1f67d8125895ea396b1acc241&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1bce80dce3f6e46fbf15b845bbded504&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-98ccf18b3f007cbd17c7784ea27d4cca</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1768781055</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Garage Entrance Setback">
      <data key="d0">Garage Entrance Setback</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The Garage Entrance Setback refers to specific planning regulations regarding the positioning of garage entrances in relation to property lines and streets.&lt;SEP&gt;Garage entrance setback refers to the required distance between a garage entrance and the street lot line as specified in regulations.&lt;SEP&gt;Garage Entrance Setback refers to regulations determining the positioning of garage entrances relative to property lines.&lt;SEP&gt;Garage Entrance Setback is the minimum distance measured from a street lot line to the entrance of a garage or carport.&lt;SEP&gt;Garage entrance setback is measured from a street lot line to the entrance of a garage or carport, indicating minimum driveway length.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-4ca2cc6a137a6b419b9a37152153b673&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b65a020b68966aec020e3367f56ac854&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a7aa2e9895420bcb5392c9333db8bab4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1ad3df252c10f7d3d9869375a3dd70b5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-652d83d3265624b311b9bf8c778fdd43</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1768781055</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Figure 130-1">
      <data key="d0">Figure 130-1</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">Figure 130-1 illustrates the step-down height limits adjacent to residential zones as per the regulations.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a966c4eaabe25aca0a1be25bd188299e</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
