    <node id="Chapter 33.130">
      <data key="d0">Chapter 33.130</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Chapter 33.130 is a set of municipal regulations that primarily pertains to Planning and Zoning, specifically focusing on Commercial and Mixed Use Zones. This chapter encompasses a wide range of regulations and guidelines that govern zoning, planning, and development activities within designated areas.

A significant aspect of Chapter 33.130 includes detailed provisions regarding height standards applicable in zoning contexts. These height regulations are essential for maintaining the intended character and functionality of different zones. Furthermore, the chapter outlines specific requirements for building setbacks, which apply to both residential and commercial uses. These setback regulations are vital for ensuring sufficient space around buildings for various uses, enhancing safety and accessibility.

In addition to setbacks, Chapter 33.130 addresses the arrangement of garage entrances in commercial and mixed-use developments. Such standards are intended to promote efficient land use while accommodating the needs of both vehicles and pedestrians.

The chapter also comprehensively covers multiple facets concerning land use, including provisions for accessory structures and fencing in specified zoning areas. Its scope includes regulations for zoning, demolition, and other development standards relevant to Commercial and Mixed Use Zones, aimed at fostering orderly and sustainable development within municipal boundaries.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0db595deea447329ffb950dc6a8bf8b3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a966c4eaabe25aca0a1be25bd188299e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3b0bbb62dc601b56a2d362f9a82f2ea6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-58ceb0639cc3b4b602e0b2c747a13e8e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f8dedd6570aca440bb011dbffebca8f0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-652d83d3265624b311b9bf8c778fdd43&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f9bdd0a72bd1cfe3ef9d36a7ad4b1bbb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b0460f02d0e013f6aa12de53db834a01</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1768781066</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Additional Development Standards for Shared Courts, Common Greens, and Alleys in Private Ownership">
      <data key="d0">Additional Development Standards for Shared Courts, Common Greens, and Alleys in Private Ownership</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">These standards apply to shared spaces in neighborhoods to promote aesthetics and usage.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-40b451e83bd236d5610bd2be4f5e2e51</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1768781066</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Institutional Development Standards">
      <data key="d0">Institutional Development Standards</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Institutional development standards regulate building practices for institutional uses in residential areas.&lt;SEP&gt;Institutional Development Standards are specified in section 33.110.270 and relate to the minimum setbacks for institutional uses within zoning regulations.&lt;SEP&gt;Institutional Development Standards guide the development requirements for institutional uses within residential zones.&lt;SEP&gt;Institutional Development Standards provide guidelines for the development and use of institutional properties to ensure compliance with zoning laws.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-40b451e83bd236d5610bd2be4f5e2e51&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-24a0dc5a40ce1a7a9401f80aac49c163&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-619bfdcc25aa9b9b415e2291e2bb9041&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-72db2074515977339664a4c6fb188af1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-07f772c2b474f898ac2fe23797d82b3d</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1768781067</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Demolitions">
      <data key="d0">Demolitions</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Demolition regulations govern the procedures and necessities for tearing down structures.&lt;SEP&gt;Demolitions refer to the regulations governing the breakdown or removal of existing structures.&lt;SEP&gt;Demolitions refer to regulatory standards surrounding the destruction of existing structures on sites designated for new development.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-40b451e83bd236d5610bd2be4f5e2e51&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0ce4a0b57a87c710f0ef76d5570894bf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b2003f93c2ff07e93de1875ffe1520e0</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1768781067</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Signs">
      <data key="d0">Signs</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Sign regulations dictate where and how signs can be placed in residential areas.&lt;SEP&gt;Signs denote the rules and regulations regarding the placement and specifications of signage in a designated area.&lt;SEP&gt;Signs refers to regulations pertaining to the display of advertising and informational messages.&lt;SEP&gt;Signs are regulated displays within zoning codes that communicate information related to businesses or properties.&lt;SEP&gt;Signs refer to various markers or indicators installed in compliance with certain specifications.&lt;SEP&gt;Signs are regulated within the Marquam Hill Plan District to ensure adequate communication and safety.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-40b451e83bd236d5610bd2be4f5e2e51&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-62277f559200167b441652bb61e8226f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-72db2074515977339664a4c6fb188af1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0ce4a0b57a87c710f0ef76d5570894bf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2474dfd41e2575d0e3e9c1417c737dbb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b2003f93c2ff07e93de1875ffe1520e0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-79f19e6435928fe4a325e8a1a4cc5dd1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4e75ce8cc1ed34d85d9e574add4504b6</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1768781067</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Recycling Areas">
      <data key="d0">Recycling Areas</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Recycling area regulations establish guidelines for the placement and maintenance of recycling facilities.&lt;SEP&gt;Recycling Areas specify requirements for designated spaces involving waste sorting and recycling practices.&lt;SEP&gt;Recycling Areas are designated zones within developments for managing recyclable materials.&lt;SEP&gt;Recycling areas are regulated by planning requirements to ensure sustainability in businesses and multifamily complexes.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-40b451e83bd236d5610bd2be4f5e2e51&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0ce4a0b57a87c710f0ef76d5570894bf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b2003f93c2ff07e93de1875ffe1520e0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4e75ce8cc1ed34d85d9e574add4504b6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-495efc01559ae21a68e9da3b57998769</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1768781067</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Neighborhood Contact I">
      <data key="d0">Neighborhood Contact I</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Neighborhood Contact I refers to outreach steps required when proposed development will add between 10,000 and 25,000 square feet of net building area to a site.&lt;SEP&gt;Neighborhood Contact I is a specific requirement for outreach steps that must occur before certain developments that add 10,000 to 25,000 square feet of building area can proceed.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-40b451e83bd236d5610bd2be4f5e2e51&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-45d67d6ae3fefc0ae11f57c206f31b06</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1768781067</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Neighborhood Contact II">
      <data key="d0">Neighborhood Contact II</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Neighborhood Contact II outlines the outreach process required when proposed development exceeds 25,000 square feet of net building area.&lt;SEP&gt;Neighborhood Contact II is a requirement for outreach steps before developments that exceed 25,000 square feet of building area can be submitted for approval.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-40b451e83bd236d5610bd2be4f5e2e51&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-45d67d6ae3fefc0ae11f57c206f31b06</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1768781067</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Flag Lot">
      <data key="d0">Flag Lot</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Flag Lot is a type of lot characterized by its width being measured at the midpoint of the flag portion.&lt;SEP&gt;A Flag Lot is a type of residential lot configuration with specific standards outlined in Title 33, often designed to maintain privacy and compatibility with surrounding properties.&lt;SEP&gt;A Flag Lot is a type of lot that has specific access requirements and must adhere to regulations regarding its dimensions and connections to streets.&lt;SEP&gt;A flag lot is a type of lot that has a long, narrow access pole connecting it to the street, subject to specific regulations.&lt;SEP&gt;The Flag Lot is a specific type of lot characterized by a flag-like shape, which has specific requirements for area, width, and zoning regulations.&lt;SEP&gt;Flag Lot is characterized by having two distinct parts (the flag and the pole), with only the flag serving as the building site.&lt;SEP&gt;A flag lot is a lot with two distinct parts: a flag, serving as the building site, and a pole, connecting it to the street.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-fd49cb12bde2e181e43e4b3ea44c114c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a18871dd80f2b9b263d128e9803fd70d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3da64931c37bc8d61d93781cf02db2d7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-59fe4540439f409de13002eb40172d2d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8b8e38ccecc8aee043d4f983d468a91b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a117438300054dc6a03e64e7a5e6bdcb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0ab589ad2a81c7f7964b172f2ba2949a</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1768781067</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Residential Setbacks">
      <data key="d0">Residential Setbacks</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Residential setbacks are building restrictions measured from property lines that dictate where structures can be built on a lot.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-fd49cb12bde2e181e43e4b3ea44c114c</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1768781067</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Landscape Buffer">
      <data key="d0">Landscape Buffer</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The landscape buffer is a required vegetated area around flag lots intended to enhance privacy and separation from adjacent properties.&lt;SEP&gt;Landscape Buffer refers to the required landscaped area separating different zoning classifications, particularly residential and commercial zones.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-fd49cb12bde2e181e43e4b3ea44c114c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f8dedd6570aca440bb011dbffebca8f0</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1768781067</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Garage Wall">
      <data key="d0">Garage Wall</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The garage wall refers to the part of the garage that faces the street and has specific length restrictions.&lt;SEP&gt;The garage wall is a structure that may have specific placement rules according to Title 33, particularly concerning street-facing facades and setbacks.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-fd49cb12bde2e181e43e4b3ea44c114c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c6c39c2445529fc5f3c446d8b193bcf5</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1768781067</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
