    </node>
    <node id="Revocable Permits">
      <data key="d0">Revocable Permits</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Revocable Permits are permissions granted for specific land uses that may change or be revoked based on new regulations or conditions.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-afe903e400770e09111a01b6ada75d31</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1768781068</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Impact Mitigation Plans">
      <data key="d0">Impact Mitigation Plans</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Impact Mitigation Plans propose strategies to mitigate negative impacts from developments.&lt;SEP&gt;Impact Mitigation Plans outline measures to alleviate negative effects arising from land use developments.&lt;SEP&gt;Impact Mitigation Plans are plans that may establish conditions of approval which include expiration dates for certain land uses.&lt;SEP&gt;Impact Mitigation Plans detail measures to address potential negative impacts of development.&lt;SEP&gt;Impact Mitigation Plans are specific plans designed to address the impacts of development in institutional zones, ensuring compatibility with surrounding residential areas.&lt;SEP&gt;Impact Mitigation Plans refer to plans or strategies designed to minimize the adverse effects of development activities on surrounding areas and communities.&lt;SEP&gt;Impact Mitigation Plans are regulatory frameworks that outline how to manage the effects of development on the environment and community.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-afe903e400770e09111a01b6ada75d31&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dd5e24f7c98b2b609a3ed6656b608420&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-17a8112955d64121a20078f6914b8018&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-93a263606fc895d08c7f7b3c2d745536&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4670488f256f231024de0291feeec277&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-594964a698a9063f34a66127a99f84d2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-98ccf18b3f007cbd17c7784ea27d4cca</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1768781068</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Building Setback">
      <data key="d0">Building Setback</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Building Setback is the distance that a building must be set back from property lines or streets, governed by local zoning regulations.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6e17537bd24336b2dd65681e37f4d9aa</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1768781068</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="City Walkway">
      <data key="d0">City Walkway</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">City Walkways are paths or sidewalks located within urban areas, often integrated into pedestrian-friendly planning.&lt;SEP&gt;A City Walkway is a designated pedestrian pathway intended to enhance walkability and connectivity in urban areas.&lt;SEP&gt;City Walkway is a designated pedestrian path that is integral to the urban design, influencing building placement and community interaction.&lt;SEP&gt;City Walkway refers to a type of walkway in urban design that facilitates pedestrian movement.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6e17537bd24336b2dd65681e37f4d9aa&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c05d0565960f8c21b2582374b4856a38&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1e3fdad639580e09c9504162fba29af8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e8a638cacad1df6f7e7b142a2867c220</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1768781068</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Maximum Setback Standard">
      <data key="d0">Maximum Setback Standard</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Maximum Setback Standard refers to the regulatory requirement that dictates how far structures must be set back from property lines or streets.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6e17537bd24336b2dd65681e37f4d9aa</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1768781068</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Street Frontages">
      <data key="d0">Street Frontages</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Street Frontages are the sides of a lot or property that face a street, influencing building placement and zoning standards.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6e17537bd24336b2dd65681e37f4d9aa</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1768781068</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Transit Streets">
      <data key="d0">Transit Streets</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Transit Streets are streets designated for public transportation use that have specific zoning standards applicable to adjacent properties.&lt;SEP&gt;Transit Streets are roadways designated to accommodate public transportation and enhance pedestrian circulation.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6e17537bd24336b2dd65681e37f4d9aa&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5e8491ef6a94a83bd06029a3a52c0a6e</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1768781068</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Outdoor Shelter">
      <data key="d0">Outdoor Shelter</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">An outdoor shelter is defined with specific requirements regarding size, operational hours, supervision, and screening from adjacent properties.&lt;SEP&gt;Outdoor Shelters are collective individual shelters in outdoor settings managed by public or non-profit agencies.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-253ac235ab4d20d7cfd5ecc312a981ef&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c0278556ab1bb29e843d8346fe04d58f</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1768781068</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="L2 Standards">
      <data key="d0">L2 Standards</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">L2 Standards refer to specific landscaping and screening standards that outdoor shelters must comply with.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-253ac235ab4d20d7cfd5ecc312a981ef</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1768781068</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="F1 Standards">
      <data key="d0">F1 Standards</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">F1 Standards refer to alternative landscaping and screening standards applicable to outdoor shelters.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-253ac235ab4d20d7cfd5ecc312a981ef</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1768781068</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="L3 Standards">
      <data key="d0">L3 Standards</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The L3 standards refer to specific requirements for landscaping and screening to maintain compliance in development projects.&lt;SEP&gt;L3 Standards are additional landscaping and screening requirements that must be followed for outdoor shelters from adjacent properties.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-253ac235ab4d20d7cfd5ecc312a981ef&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4328dc69e2d2b9998d2666c8193b0245</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1768781069</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="3,000 Square Feet">
      <data key="d0">3,000 Square Feet</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">3,000 square feet is the minimum site size required for an outdoor shelter.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-253ac235ab4d20d7cfd5ecc312a981ef</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1768781069</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="2 Acres">
      <data key="d0">2 Acres</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">2 acres is the maximum size for an outdoor shelter located in I zones.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-253ac235ab4d20d7cfd5ecc312a981ef</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1768781069</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="5 Feet">
      <data key="d0">5 Feet</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">5 feet is the required setback distance for development associated with the outdoor shelter from all lot lines.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-253ac235ab4d20d7cfd5ecc312a981ef</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1768781069</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="20 Feet">
      <data key="d0">20 Feet</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">20 feet is the maximum height allowed for structures associated with the outdoor shelter.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-253ac235ab4d20d7cfd5ecc312a981ef</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1768781069</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Water Facilities">
      <data key="d0">Water Facilities</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Water facilities refer to construction projects that must adhere to regulations regarding proximity to wetlands and water bodies.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c8ead15b7371d315a50edc3463b2359a</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1768781069</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Wetland">
      <data key="d0">Wetland</data>
      <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
      <data key="d2">A Wetland is a specific type of natural feature that must be considered in planning and zoning to protect aquatic ecosystems and regulate development.&lt;SEP&gt;Wetlands are ecological areas that must be respected in construction, with specific regulations preventing development within 50 feet.&lt;SEP&gt;Wetland is an area of land where water covers the soil, either permanently or seasonally, supporting aquatic plants and wildlife.&lt;SEP&gt;Wetlands possess functional values such as stormwater detention and providing food and shelter for migrating waterfowl.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c8ead15b7371d315a50edc3463b2359a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-04cb5d072ac6b2a3e906facf4052c5aa&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-92d544a559cce4659ea9ffd7dad0732c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-94d2f2ba51a73a23de500a4e54b5f682</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1768781069</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Table 430-5">
      <data key="d0">Table 430-5</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">Table 430-5 outlines maximum roadway widths and right-of-way requirements to be met for development projects.&lt;SEP&gt;Table 430-5 lists maximum right-of-way and roadway widths for different types of street base zones.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c8ead15b7371d315a50edc3463b2359a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4c2325c884efc6a13f1b84583326ff8d</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1768781069</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Table 430-6">
      <data key="d0">Table 430-6</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">Table 430-6 specifies the minimum area required outside of resource areas for different zoning classifications.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c8ead15b7371d315a50edc3463b2359a</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1768781069</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Property Line Adjustments">
      <data key="d0">Property Line Adjustments</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Property Line Adjustments involve changing the boundaries of parcels as per land use and ownership requirements.&lt;SEP&gt;Property line adjustments are regulatory processes for redefining property boundaries in compliance with environmental standards.&lt;SEP&gt;Property Line Adjustments refer to modifications made to property specifications, including the processing of various activities related to land use.&lt;SEP&gt;Property Line Adjustments (PLAs) refer to the standards and practices for modifying the boundaries of properties in specific zones.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c8ead15b7371d315a50edc3463b2359a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c232ce107cc5a0bd00a23892d69d0561&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f5a5816615430d9b588c4adaf4aa3131&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dd5e24f7c98b2b609a3ed6656b608420</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1768781069</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Map 430-10">
      <data key="d0">Map 430-10</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">Map 430-10 illustrates areas subject to specific resource enhancement project standards and ecological considerations.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c8ead15b7371d315a50edc3463b2359a</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1768781069</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Bank Reconfiguration">
      <data key="d0">Bank Reconfiguration</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Bank reconfiguration refers to the process of modifying banks along waterways or drainage systems to improve stability and environmental conditions.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c8ead15b7371d315a50edc3463b2359a</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1768781069</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Basking Features Area">
      <data key="d0">Basking Features Area</data>
      <data key="d1">location</data>
      <data key="d2">The Basking Features Area is a designated section shown on Map 430-10 where specific bank reconfiguration standards apply.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c8ead15b7371d315a50edc3463b2359a</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1768781069</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Pleasant Valley">
      <data key="d0">Pleasant Valley</data>
      <data key="d1">location</data>
      <data key="d2">Pleasant Valley is a geographic area that is the focus of natural resource management and overlay regulations.&lt;SEP&gt;Pleasant Valley refers to the geographical area encompassed by the Pleasant Valley Natural Resources Overlay Zone, which is subject to specific land use regulations.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-2762500c7c689febd52ecacda4e0e39d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bca9af7fb86386b5d8e07a8895278701</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1768781069</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Pleasant Valley Natural Resources Protection Plan">
      <data key="d0">Pleasant Valley Natural Resources Protection Plan</data>
      <data key="d1">document</data>
      <data key="d2">The Pleasant Valley Natural Resources Protection Plan outlines the significant natural resources within Pleasant Valley and the city's policy objectives.&lt;SEP&gt;The Pleasant Valley Natural Resources Protection Plan outlines significant resources and functional values for impact evaluations.&lt;SEP&gt;The Pleasant Valley Natural Resources Protection Plan details identified resources and functional values in the overlay zone.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-2762500c7c689febd52ecacda4e0e39d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-51dc10f7cf6f3ff9bea2eb170cf1c4dc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0c4fc40bc0c1ae23128f4ba53742ed42</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1768781069</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="33.465 Standards">
      <data key="d0">33.465 Standards</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The 33.465 standards encompass regulations and criteria for land management, resource enhancement, and development within Pleasant Valley.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-2762500c7c689febd52ecacda4e0e39d</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1768781069</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Nuisance Plant List">
      <data key="d0">Nuisance Plant List</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">The Nuisance Plant List outlines plant species deemed undesirable and subject to removal regulations.&lt;SEP&gt;The Nuisance Plant List is a catalog of plant species that are considered undesirable and can be removed in accordance with local regulations.&lt;SEP&gt;The Nuisance Plant List details non-native plants that may be regulated under the Pleasant Valley natural resource policies.&lt;SEP&gt;The Nuisance Plant List consists of plants that are prohibited from being planted or propagated.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-2762500c7c689febd52ecacda4e0e39d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-20addac2bd7bf67d0c780bffff45e581&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bcdc5413a0ad23895c5bdcf3326ac8a4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5e28ba6f352cf461311b1a12b892a5ff</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1768781069</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Pleasant Valley Natural Resources Management Plans">
      <data key="d0">Pleasant Valley Natural Resources Management Plans</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Pleasant Valley Natural Resources Management Plans are intended to outline the purpose, scope, procedure, and components of resource management in Pleasant Valley.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-2762500c7c689febd52ecacda4e0e39d</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1768781069</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Pleasant Valley Natural Resources Report">
      <data key="d0">Pleasant Valley Natural Resources Report</data>
      <data key="d1">document</data>
      <data key="d2">The Pleasant Valley Natural Resources Report details the application of the Pleasant Valley Natural Resources Overlay Zone and involves an inventory of significant natural resources.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-2762500c7c689febd52ecacda4e0e39d</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1768781069</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Subparagraph 33.465.165.A.2.c">
      <data key="d0">Subparagraph 33.465.165.A.2.c</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">Subparagraph 33.465.165.A.2.c specifies minimum lot dimension requirements for flag lots in the Pleasant Valley area.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a18871dd80f2b9b263d128e9803fd70d</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1768781069</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Minimum Lot Dimensions">
      <data key="d0">Minimum Lot Dimensions</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Minimum lot dimensions refer to the required dimensions that flag lots must meet according to local zoning regulations.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a18871dd80f2b9b263d128e9803fd70d</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1768781069</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Environmental Resource Areas">
      <data key="d0">Environmental Resource Areas</data>
      <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
      <data key="d2">Environmental resource areas are protected zones that require careful consideration during land development to minimize impact.&lt;SEP&gt;Environmental Resource Areas within the PIR Plan District provide habitats for wildlife and are designed for passive recreational uses.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a18871dd80f2b9b263d128e9803fd70d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dd02a036b3524dda605ba42dd4a99988</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1768781069</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Duplexes">
      <data key="d0">Duplexes</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Duplexes are residential buildings consisting of two separate living units, serving as a standard example in zoning discussions.&lt;SEP&gt;Duplexes are residential units consisting of two separate living spaces and are exempt from certain provisions of the zoning regulations.&lt;SEP&gt;Duplexes are a housing type that may be requested within the RF through R2.5 zones in a Planned Development.&lt;SEP&gt;Duplexes are residential buildings that consist of two living units and are subject to zoning regulations within the Pleasant Valley Plan District.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a18871dd80f2b9b263d128e9803fd70d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c6c39c2445529fc5f3c446d8b193bcf5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-28ffc992b5e373540a3ee78f16308e61&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a7282e754cd453819f3e67c09c8e7a91</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1768781069</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Triplexes">
      <data key="d0">Triplexes</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Triplexes are residential buildings comprising three separate living units, mentioned within zoning regulations.&lt;SEP&gt;Triplexes are residential buildings containing three separate living units and are exempt from the regulations outlined in Chapter 33.130 Title 33.&lt;SEP&gt;Triplexes are residential buildings that consist of three living units and are regulated under the zoning laws applicable to the Pleasant Valley Plan District.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a18871dd80f2b9b263d128e9803fd70d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c6c39c2445529fc5f3c446d8b193bcf5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a7282e754cd453819f3e67c09c8e7a91</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1768781069</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Fourplexes">
      <data key="d0">Fourplexes</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Fourplexes are residential structures with four distinct living units, serving as a focal point for setback standards in zoning.&lt;SEP&gt;Fourplexes are residential structures that consist of four units and are not required to meet specific zoning provisions.&lt;SEP&gt;Fourplexes are residential buildings comprising four living units and are included in the zoning regulations for the Pleasant Valley Plan District.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a18871dd80f2b9b263d128e9803fd70d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c6c39c2445529fc5f3c446d8b193bcf5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a7282e754cd453819f3e67c09c8e7a91</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1768781069</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Multi-Dwelling Structures">
      <data key="d0">Multi-Dwelling Structures</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Multi-dwelling structures are residential buildings containing multiple housing units, subject to specific zoning standards.&lt;SEP&gt;Multi-Dwelling Structures refer to housing involving multiple units that may be requested in specified zones under Planned Development.&lt;SEP&gt;Multi-dwelling structures encompass residential buildings that contain multiple housing units and are prohibited in certain developments within the RF zone.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a18871dd80f2b9b263d128e9803fd70d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cbb810e875d66f848b351a4f1271e2a7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-28ffc992b5e373540a3ee78f16308e61</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1768781069</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Revocable Permit">
      <data key="d0">Revocable Permit</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">A revocable permit allows temporary use of property with specific conditions, including eligibility for extensions under certain criteria.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-187fab99be5d8e40e38df505230ff13b</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1768781069</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Legal Status of Lots">
      <data key="d0">Legal Status of Lots</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The legal status of lots refers to their standing as legal properties based on various criteria, including recorded plats and zoning regulations.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-187fab99be5d8e40e38df505230ff13b</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1768781069</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Type IIx Procedure">
      <data key="d0">Type IIx Procedure</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">The Type IIx Procedure is a regulatory process designed for land division reviews, specifically targeting smaller proposals typically involving four to ten lots. This procedure establishes defined parameters that applicants must adhere to, differentiating it from other types of land use reviews, such as the Type II designation. The Type IIx Procedure is intended for processing minor amendments to approved Planned Unit Developments, focusing primarily on land divisions.

One of the key features of the Type IIx Procedure is its provision for extended decision-making time compared to the Type II process, allowing for more thorough assessments. It encompasses multiple criteria and reviews throughout the approval process, ensuring that various aspects of land use regulations are considered.

Moreover, the Type IIx Procedure allows for appeals against decisions made by the Director of Planning, Permitting, and Development (PP&amp;D), enabling applicants to seek reviews from another decision-making body. This procedure serves as a specialized classification within land use reviews, activated under certain conditions that necessitate a specific approach to application processing. Each of these elements contributes to a nuanced understanding of the Type IIx Procedure, clarifying its role within the broader regulatory framework governing land development.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d855fbf99c0d0379fc75e502d7bd623a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b9bda12d4c6ac60dd023d2422e670f07&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-07e86c208ee49379750e080df5cf9722&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ac7f584fe857b5fce6394c52a4277eee&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f464bb6caf4196f9f098e5debb9f7fc4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4e5352641a4a29255f045f1ffa49de30&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-614d099bcb8d71cfc787e0cbccc1ebbf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f63396adc71a2e9185adb3bc7b8db5ad&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3bde6fb397c7366841124f779d183b6c</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1768781069</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Nonconforming Use Regulations">
      <data key="d0">Nonconforming Use Regulations</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Nonconforming use regulations dictate how properties that do not comply with current zoning can be treated under certain conditions.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-187fab99be5d8e40e38df505230ff13b</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1768781069</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Nonconforming Residential Density Regulations">
      <data key="d0">Nonconforming Residential Density Regulations</data>
