    <node id="Pre-Application Conference">
      <data key="d0">Pre-Application Conference</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">The Pre-Application Conference is a required meeting before applying for an ELD review.&lt;SEP&gt;Pre-Application Conference is a mandatory meeting for all land division requests that must occur before applying for an ELD review.&lt;SEP&gt;The Pre-Application Conference is an optional meeting related to specific land use review requirements.&lt;SEP&gt;Pre-Application Conference is a meeting to inform applicants about the requirements and facilitate information exchange regarding land use proposals.&lt;SEP&gt;The Pre-Application Conference is a meeting that reviews the application process and requirements before formal submission.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3f69badbdc0a01b4a234db008584a915&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-07e86c208ee49379750e080df5cf9722&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c4263b41e43a7e8a83f6017fb2cf1c50&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-614d099bcb8d71cfc787e0cbccc1ebbf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a5f5ada1bd73ae6d3ab8a5bb43ff6e2b</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1768781071</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="ELD Review">
      <data key="d0">ELD Review</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">The ELD Review is a process that applications must undergo to assess land use proposals.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3f69badbdc0a01b4a234db008584a915</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1768781071</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="I Zone">
      <data key="d0">I Zone</data>
      <data key="d1">location</data>
      <data key="d2">The I Zone is a designated regulatory area primarily characterized by its industrial zoning classification, which imposes specific limitations on various uses, particularly regarding Household Living and Group Living uses as set forth in Title 33 regulations. Within this zone, residential uses are generally prohibited to maintain the industrial focus, ensuring that the area's primary function is aligned with industrial activities.

Additionally, the I Zone permits certain commercial operations, such as drive-through facilities, provided these align with existing zoning regulations. The zone includes specific stipulations related to parking requirements that are comparable to those established in the Employment General (EG) Zone, facilitating access while sustaining operational efficiency for industrial and related uses.

Prohibitions within the I Zone extend to certain types of shelters, including mass shelters, to uphold the industrial integrity of the area. However, there are conditional regulations for outdoor shelters, which require adherence to established criteria during the approval process. The I Zone also encompasses regulations that pertain to the installation and management of wind turbines, reflecting a growing consideration for renewable energy sources within industrial contexts.

Further clarifying its purpose, the I Zone supports manufacturing and heavy industry, ensuring the area is equipped to accommodate various nonconforming uses while upholding the zoning's primary industrial focus. Overall, the I Zone is a crucial regulatory area designed to delineate industrial activities from residential uses and ensure appropriate controls are in place for both land use and development.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f8966d01e665f9cb17191db9bb5912d2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c12d6555e1901995a51aaf68fcae440f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-221d1ae9cf50b3236ae14ae949934120&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9ac355b146a97e19880b3790c5ffe83a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3caa6629bdef153dada4d72aed50d85b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a7c158baba6b66776b2fa39c55edd02f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6e927f3c64b3afc1f943e861ae4fac76&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5814020534bf1fbc0a6e4c64746ca31d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0b4cc500f0cf6ffd63a8f594c9055457</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1768781071</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Urban Growth Boundary (UGB)">
      <data key="d0">Urban Growth Boundary (UGB)</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The Urban Growth Boundary is a demarcation that impacts property owner notifications and land use regulations.&lt;SEP&gt;The Urban Growth Boundary (UGB) is a designated area that impacts how applications and notices are processed based on location.&lt;SEP&gt;The Urban Growth Boundary (UGB) delineates areas where development is encouraged and controlled.&lt;SEP&gt;The Urban Growth Boundary (UGB) delineates the areas for urban development and influences notification procedures for land use applications.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3f69badbdc0a01b4a234db008584a915&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c4263b41e43a7e8a83f6017fb2cf1c50&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4e8e06f7fcece5e199a44dcbdcbf9d97&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b60dcf59d4eb9520c7bf8e9968ecf967</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1768781071</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Notice of Decision">
      <data key="d0">Notice of Decision</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The Notice of Decision is a formal notification mailed after the Director of PP&amp;D makes a final decision regarding an application.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3f69badbdc0a01b4a234db008584a915</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1768781071</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Appeal">
      <data key="d0">Appeal</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">An Appeal is a formal request to review the decision made by the Director of PP&amp;D, submitted by the applicant or other interested parties.&lt;SEP&gt;The appeal process allows individuals to contest the decisions made by the Director of PP&amp;D within a specified timeframe.&lt;SEP&gt;An Appeal is the formal process by which decisions made by the review body can be contested by stakeholders within a specified timeframe.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3f69badbdc0a01b4a234db008584a915&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-644cc4e15ec9d767b69917b94f282c47&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4e8e06f7fcece5e199a44dcbdcbf9d97</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1768781071</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Land Use Decisions">
      <data key="d0">Land Use Decisions</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Land Use Decisions encompass various determinations made concerning land use applications and appeals.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3f69badbdc0a01b4a234db008584a915</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1768781071</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="City Agencies">
      <data key="d0">City Agencies</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">City agencies include various governmental bodies that may be consulted during the land use review process to gather relevant information.&lt;SEP&gt;City Agencies are public organizations that may be consulted by the Director of PP&amp;D for information relevant to requests.&lt;SEP&gt;City Agencies are local government organizations that may be consulted by the Director of PP&amp;D during the decision-making process.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3f69badbdc0a01b4a234db008584a915&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c4263b41e43a7e8a83f6017fb2cf1c50&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-200b9b34fa908e42e599f92db53639cf</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1768781071</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Ownership">
      <data key="d0">Ownership</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Ownership signifies one or more contiguous lots owned by a single entity or partnership.&lt;SEP&gt;Ownership is defined as one or more contiguous lots owned by the same entity, which can include individuals, partnerships, associations, or corporations.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-58d46c43c9570caf791239d0c34502c3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c0278556ab1bb29e843d8346fe04d58f</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1768781071</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Parking Area">
      <data key="d0">Parking Area</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">A Parking Area is the designated space for the standing and circulation of motor vehicles.&lt;SEP&gt;A parking area is designated space for the standing and maneuvering of motor vehicles, excluding driveways.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-58d46c43c9570caf791239d0c34502c3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c0278556ab1bb29e843d8346fe04d58f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-86420cc97941914b0b1ab8dfd5d58107</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1768781071</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Parking Space">
      <data key="d0">Parking Space</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">A parking space is an area specifically designated for parking a motor vehicle.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-58d46c43c9570caf791239d0c34502c3</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1768781071</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Peace Officer">
      <data key="d0">Peace Officer</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">A Peace Officer includes members of law enforcement such as the Oregon State Police and local sheriffs, who have authority to enforce the law.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-58d46c43c9570caf791239d0c34502c3</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1768781071</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Pedestrian Access Route">
      <data key="d0">Pedestrian Access Route</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">A pedestrian access route is a pathway designed to connect the main entrance of a building to bicycle parking, ensuring safety and accessibility.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-58d46c43c9570caf791239d0c34502c3</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1768781071</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Pedestrian Connection">
      <data key="d0">Pedestrian Connection</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">A pedestrian connection designed for pedestrians and bicycles, linking to a maintained street.&lt;SEP&gt;Pedestrian connections are pathways designed for foot traffic that can affect fence placement in setbacks.&lt;SEP&gt;Pedestrian connection refers to the required link between street and main entrance of dwelling units.&lt;SEP&gt;Pedestrian connections offer access for pedestrians and cyclists between streets or from streets to amenities, typically connecting private streets or tracts.&lt;SEP&gt;A pedestrian connection is a through route for pedestrians and bicyclists connecting two streets or lots, which can accommodate vehicles as well.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-58d46c43c9570caf791239d0c34502c3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a1daea73587f156d45df1e0850a2216e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ce84b33b7ab639d2d53ae06b94dc49e4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cc4ad1e18166e18f3ffc6869780387e3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4271bb9cc0287dcb0b401b760eb4864e</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1768781071</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Phased Development Plan">
      <data key="d0">Phased Development Plan</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">A phased development plan outlines the step-by-step development of a site, including phases, proposed uses, and timelines.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-58d46c43c9570caf791239d0c34502c3</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1768781071</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Planning and Sustainability Director">
      <data key="d0">Planning and Sustainability Director</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">The Planning and Sustainability Director is responsible for making nominations for advisory board membership and is involved in staffing the board.&lt;SEP&gt;The Planning and Sustainability Director is responsible for providing notice to individuals regarding public hearings.&lt;SEP&gt;The Planning and Sustainability Director is an official responsible for initiating text amendments related to land use.&lt;SEP&gt;The Planning and Sustainability Director oversees the City of Portland Bureau of Planning and Sustainability.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-58d46c43c9570caf791239d0c34502c3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a451e7e8d08a7ee4503b05d9e1fac269&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-599508fe86fb4e6ee4dd67e02ddb8b50&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4f231106269a74d76750e77d25977faa</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1768781071</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Plat">
      <data key="d0">Plat</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">A plat consists of diagrams and writings that describe the details of a land division, including partition and subdivision.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-58d46c43c9570caf791239d0c34502c3</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1768781072</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Pollution Reduction Facility">
      <data key="d0">Pollution Reduction Facility</data>
      <data key="d1">artifact</data>
      <data key="d2">A pollution reduction facility is designed to remove contaminants from stormwater, often utilizing native plants for integration into the environment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-58d46c43c9570caf791239d0c34502c3</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1768781072</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Potential Landslide Hazard Area">
      <data key="d0">Potential Landslide Hazard Area</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">A Potential Landslide Hazard Area is a designated region where landslide risks are identified, impacting land-use decisions.&lt;SEP&gt;Potential Landslide Hazard Area signifies locations that may be at risk for landslides, affecting land division considerations.&lt;SEP&gt;The potential landslide hazard area indicates locations at risk of landslides, requiring adherence to certain regulations.&lt;SEP&gt;Potential Landslide Hazard Area is a specific geographic zone where development is regulated due to the risk of landslides.&lt;SEP&gt;Potential Landslide Hazard Areas are regions identified as at risk for landslides, as indicated on the city's mapping tools.&lt;SEP&gt;A potential landslide hazard area is indicated on a map by the City, identifying regions at risk for landslides.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-58d46c43c9570caf791239d0c34502c3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b9bda12d4c6ac60dd023d2422e670f07&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-38a0672dd2aa59189e1e13c242175d7b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9cfe18720ad9b5bf5dadab610a40b064&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f464bb6caf4196f9f098e5debb9f7fc4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-80424e1fdfc525e03fb931cf5e6d14ed</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1768781072</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Lot">
      <data key="d0">Lot</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">A lot refers to a parcel of land with specific exemptions regarding slope and area that may not require compliance with visitable standards.&lt;SEP&gt;A Lot refers to a defined parcel of land that meets specific regulations for confirmation as part of land use procedures.&lt;SEP&gt;Lot refers to a parcel of land defined for a specific use or development.&lt;SEP&gt;Lot refers to a defined area of land designated for a specific use or development, related to urban planning.&lt;SEP&gt;A lot is a legally defined piece of land resulting from a land division, excluding tracts.&lt;SEP&gt;A lot refers to a defined parcel of land that can be owned or developed according to zoning regulations.&lt;SEP&gt;A defined parcel of land on which calculations for slope, area, and other measurements are made based on its dimensions and elevations.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-58d46c43c9570caf791239d0c34502c3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0f1548067bb4833113c6a54b4a7e87fc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a421dc7c5c5ed797ef20bf18c33cb13c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-63c21c957d68787ba4fea52897f3a3b8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a117438300054dc6a03e64e7a5e6bdcb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0ab589ad2a81c7f7964b172f2ba2949a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-94d2f2ba51a73a23de500a4e54b5f682</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1768781072</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Driveway">
      <data key="d0">Driveway</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">A Driveway provides vehicular access and circulation to a site and does not include parking areas or maneuvering spaces.&lt;SEP&gt;A driveway is a private road allowing access to the property, typically leading from the street to a garage or parking space.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-58d46c43c9570caf791239d0c34502c3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-142263ca6fb69f507db833e40c26eab3</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1768781072</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Garage">
      <data key="d0">Garage</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">A garage is a structure for housing vehicles, which must comply with specific regulations regarding visibility and size.&lt;SEP&gt;Garages are structures intended for parking vehicles that must comply with outlined standards related to visibility, setbacks, and positioning relative to the street.&lt;SEP&gt;A Garage refers to an enclosed structure used for parking vehicles and often for storage or workspace.&lt;SEP&gt;A garage is a covered structure designed to provide shelter for vehicles, connected to a right-of-way by a driveway.&lt;SEP&gt;A garage is a shelter for vehicles, often attached to or part of a building.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-58d46c43c9570caf791239d0c34502c3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6e340681b50bf551a0c9458d916a1b39&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-92d544a559cce4659ea9ffd7dad0732c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-86420cc97941914b0b1ab8dfd5d58107&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-90656f21118fd0ad7c0ee7822eac4d60</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1768781072</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Vehicle Areas">
      <data key="d0">Vehicle Areas</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Vehicle areas encompass regions designated for vehicle access and must adhere to standards separating them from building porches and streets.&lt;SEP&gt;Vehicle Areas refer to designated spaces for motor vehicles in relation to building sites, including driveways and parking standards.&lt;SEP&gt;Vehicle areas are designated spaces for parking and traffic, subject to regulations for safety, aesthetics, and environmental impacts.&lt;SEP&gt;Vehicle Areas refer to designated zones for parking or movement of vehicles within an urban or planned environment.&lt;SEP&gt;Vehicle Areas refer to spaces designed for vehicle circulation and parking, including driveways and parking lots.&lt;SEP&gt;Vehicle areas are designated spaces for the operation, parking, and storage of motor vehicles.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-58d46c43c9570caf791239d0c34502c3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-145c2e6cf2a3147235dc3027efb2fd6d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ab6c39f118dd3cc1984138088ad67005&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a6dbd7e7cb1c5715db55af9b8b09b073&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-94d2f2ba51a73a23de500a4e54b5f682&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-606354f765e28b95d29b753fc9fe43fd</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1768781072</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="National Register of Historic Places">
      <data key="d0">National Register of Historic Places</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">The National Register of Historic Places is a federal program in the United States that identifies and protects historically significant sites and structures. It serves as a comprehensive registry listing historic districts and resources deemed worthy of preservation, including archaeological resources and landmarks of cultural importance.

Overseen by a federal keeper, the National Register involves a formal process for listing and protecting these historic sites. This designation not only acknowledges the historical significance of these locations but also establishes regulations for their preservation. The designation consequently affects various reviews and designations at local levels, including influencing local landmark designation processes and impacting resource reviews, such as those in Portland.

Additionally, the National Register's designation impacts the inclusion of these sites on Official Zoning Maps, thereby integrating historic preservation into broader urban planning and management efforts. Thus, the National Register of Historic Places plays a crucial role in the conservation of America's historical and cultural heritage.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7e0d4fb6204c8defda427d1f8fff2f0f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-851e46c6798be63a5ed200499e9b955d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5fdaeb46ee0afe6f8cbf449de5ce1578&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-505f218cdd094bc3f7a968cd178d268b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f4395c057e21d1b49701c5d6e444bef5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8034fc51f3b39a89498e8a31263eac35&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-33f6d86139e9f8ebbadc462769659fed&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-721ec91bd394c742ab66be71cc2391f1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fca333850d886eb6696137e8d84c9908&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9157cebcd51ea7cec9c5bd83c0f3df47</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1768781072</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Street Types">
      <data key="d0">Street Types</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Street types include different categories of streets like arterial streets, local service streets, and pedestrian connections.&lt;SEP&gt;Street types classify various forms of streets in urban planning, impacting traffic flow and access.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-58d46c43c9570caf791239d0c34502c3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-61e934d288b532969001ac5c602e3192</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1768781072</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Height Standards">
      <data key="d0">Height Standards</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Height standards are regulations that serve various purposes, including fostering reasonable building scale and promoting privacy for neighboring properties.&lt;SEP&gt;Height standards dictate the maximum building height in various zoning areas to manage city aesthetics and function.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3f013869c1ec32a0acb630a40f63c4cc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-133e0d785413e64759688100094b7c2e</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1768781072</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Maximum Height">
      <data key="d0">Maximum Height</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Maximum height refers to the regulatory limit on the vertical dimension permitted for buildings and structures in residential areas.&lt;SEP&gt;Maximum Height refers to the regulatory height limit for structures in specified zoning categories as defined in planning laws.&lt;SEP&gt;Maximum Height refers to the regulatory limits placed on the height of buildings based on their zoning classification, such as 30 feet, 75 feet, or 150 feet.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3f013869c1ec32a0acb630a40f63c4cc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8b8e38ccecc8aee043d4f983d468a91b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cf1cc9b5437586cfb484233e2286c341</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1768781072</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Chimneys">
      <data key="d0">Chimneys</data>
      <data key="d1">artifact</data>
      <data key="d2">Chimneys are structures attached to buildings, which may extend above height limits if they comply with specific size requirements.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3f013869c1ec32a0acb630a40f63c4cc</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1768781072</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Dormers">
      <data key="d0">Dormers</data>
      <data key="d1">artifact</data>
      <data key="d2">Dormers are structural extensions of a roof that may not be included in height calculations under certain conditions.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3f013869c1ec32a0acb630a40f63c4cc</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1768781072</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Farm Buildings">
      <data key="d0">Farm Buildings</data>
      <data key="d1">artifact</data>
      <data key="d2">Farm buildings, such as silos and barns, are exempt from height limits under specific agricultural use conditions.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3f013869c1ec32a0acb630a40f63c4cc</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1768781072</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Antennas">
      <data key="d0">Antennas</data>
      <data key="d1">artifact</data>
      <data key="d2">Antennas are structures that are exempt from maximum height limits.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3f013869c1ec32a0acb630a40f63c4cc</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1768781072</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Small Wind Turbines">
      <data key="d0">Small Wind Turbines</data>
      <data key="d1">artifact</data>
      <data key="d2">Small wind turbines are subject to specific regulations detailed in Chapter 33.299 related to their height and placement.&lt;SEP&gt;Small wind turbines are defined by an output of 10 kW or less and certified for performance standards.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3f013869c1ec32a0acb630a40f63c4cc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4ed32ffcf90280c2cd045c39203b18c7</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1768781072</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Steeply Sloping Lots">
      <data key="d0">Steeply Sloping Lots</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Steeply Sloping Lots refers to lots with a slope of 20 percent or greater, impacting building setback regulations.&lt;SEP&gt;Steeply sloping lots refer to properties with a significant downhill or uphill slope that are subject to alternative height requirements.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3f013869c1ec32a0acb630a40f63c4cc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-be5907995aac72aa1fae17ac5227927a</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1768781072</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Setback Regulations">
      <data key="d0">Setback Regulations</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Setback regulations dictate the required distances between structures and property boundaries to ensure light, air, and fire safety.&lt;SEP&gt;Setback regulations determine the required distances that structures must maintain from property lines.&lt;SEP&gt;Setback Regulations dictate the minimum distance buildings must be set back from property lines, affecting development layout.&lt;SEP&gt;Setback regulations dictate the minimum distance structures must be from property boundaries for safety and aesthetic considerations.&lt;SEP&gt;Setback Regulations dictate how far structures must be from property lines, impacting both vertical and horizontal structures.&lt;SEP&gt;Setback regulations are rules that define the minimum distance buildings must be set back from property lines or streets.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3f013869c1ec32a0acb630a40f63c4cc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-27242cf75127df0f7d45a0655595e417&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b77775bfe50a48aa700ab96a9ab1c440&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9a48525044c3c1bf75799ef0954937b7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7ab64be803cdc30ed89433c92047a024&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2574af8787db7883bc15da7cbabd3a3b</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1768781072</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Chapter 33.930">
      <data key="d0">Chapter 33.930</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Chapter 33.930 provides guidance on measurements related to setback requirements and average slope calculations in zoning.&lt;SEP&gt;Chapter 33.930 provides guidelines on measurements related to building height regulations within zoning laws.&lt;SEP&gt;Chapter 33.930 details the measurements associated with building setbacks and standards.&lt;SEP&gt;Chapter 33.930 provides guidelines related to measurements in the context of Planning and Zoning.&lt;SEP&gt;Chapter 33.930 outlines specific zoning regulations, including measuring lot widths, depths, and areas, as well as setback averaging.&lt;SEP&gt;Chapter 33.930 outlines specific measurement standards and rules related to building heights and other structures in zoning regulations.&lt;SEP&gt;Chapter 33.930 refers to a section of planning and zoning regulations that includes methodologies for measuring lot dimensions and areas.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3f013869c1ec32a0acb630a40f63c4cc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d58dbc74dfa54bc79315d9a882ce6503&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-619bfdcc25aa9b9b415e2291e2bb9041&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6feb38e3d4faebf120b7cf1e9165848c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e76f0e466ee927633a830eb76da6e9d5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a421dc7c5c5ed797ef20bf18c33cb13c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1b06e985a4445f1b158ef2343cff5b0e</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1768781072</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Visitability Standards">
      <data key="d0">Visitability Standards</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Visitability standards are regulations ensuring accessible features in housing, accommodating residents and visitors of all abilities.&lt;SEP&gt;Visitability standards are regulations ensuring accessibility features in housing, promoting inclusivity for people of all ages and abilities.&lt;SEP&gt;Visitability Standards outline requirements for accessible dwelling units in Planned Developments.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a1daea73587f156d45df1e0850a2216e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a7c158baba6b66776b2fa39c55edd02f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-924adce58a3a3a8622d21ee99005b5a7</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1768781072</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Visitability Entrance">
      <data key="d0">Visitability Entrance</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">A visitability entrance must provide accessible entry without stairs, in compliance with visitability standards.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a1daea73587f156d45df1e0850a2216e</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1768781072</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Visitable Bathroom">
      <data key="d0">Visitable Bathroom</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">A visitable bathroom designed to accommodate specific accessibility requirements, as outlined by visitability standards.&lt;SEP&gt;The visitable bathroom must be accessible and included in the dimensions that comply with visitable standards, specifically having wide door openings.&lt;SEP&gt;The Visitable Bathroom must accommodate specific space requirements for accessibility, ensuring usability for individuals with mobility challenges.&lt;SEP&gt;Visitable bathrooms are designed to accommodate accessibility requirements, including specified dimensions for the space available for maneuvering.&lt;SEP&gt;A visitable bathroom must have specific design features including a sink, toilet, and adequate unobstructed space for accessibility.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a1daea73587f156d45df1e0850a2216e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-16a962491a7cd2c102b96a01ffc8d4cd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ff254476c2742dcdf5c26a1256fd36e8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-63c21c957d68787ba4fea52897f3a3b8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-924adce58a3a3a8622d21ee99005b5a7</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1768781072</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Visitable Living Area">
      <data key="d0">Visitable Living Area</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">A visitable living area must meet size and accessibility standards in accordance with visitability guidelines.&lt;SEP&gt;The Visitable Living Area should provide adequate space for accessibility, being on the same floor as the entrance or reachable via ramps or lifts.&lt;SEP&gt;The visitable living area is a designated space that must be at least 200 square feet and located on the same floor as the visitable entrance.&lt;SEP&gt;The visitable living area is required to have a minimum size of 200 square feet and must be accessible from the visitable entrance.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a1daea73587f156d45df1e0850a2216e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-16a962491a7cd2c102b96a01ffc8d4cd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ff254476c2742dcdf5c26a1256fd36e8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-63c21c957d68787ba4fea52897f3a3b8</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1768781072</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Affordable Fourplex">
      <data key="d0">Affordable Fourplex</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">An affordable fourplex is a multi-dwelling structure with specific affordability and zoning requirements.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a1daea73587f156d45df1e0850a2216e</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1768781072</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="January 1, 2032">
      <data key="d0">January 1, 2032</data>
      <data key="d1">date</data>
      <data key="d2">January 1, 2032 is the deadline before which adjustments to minimum lot area requirements can be requested.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a1daea73587f156d45df1e0850a2216e</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1768781072</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="60 Percent of Area Median Family Income">
      <data key="d0">60 Percent of Area Median Family Income</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">This concept refers to the income threshold used to determine affordable units within the project.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a1daea73587f156d45df1e0850a2216e</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1768781072</data>
