    <node id="Group Living">
      <data key="d0">Group Living</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Group Living refers to a category of residential arrangements characterized by the accommodation of multiple occupants, often involving communal living spaces and shared facilities. Such arrangements typically involve dwelling units with more than eight bedrooms and are distinguished from traditional Household Living classifications. 

Under Title 33 regulations, Group Living is permitted for shared accommodations, but is subject to specific zoning restrictions and impact mitigation plans in designated areas. Additionally, there are size limitations that apply to these arrangements, especially for properties that were already providing Group Living services prior to July 9, 2018. When properties exceed 3,500 square feet, they may have further regulatory implications.

Group Living pertains not only to general housing situations but also encompasses specific programs that provide care and training for individuals facing psychiatric, alcohol, or drug-related challenges, as well as transitional support for former offenders. In essence, Group Living captures a broad spectrum of communal residential arrangements designed to cater to various social and support needs, fostering an environment where individuals can share living spaces while receiving necessary care or assistance.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d984c1f88be9e0bd0ee4f857384d9046&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-78724ed583edac4d2f2940a886eda8af&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0f5a24cd6c84e70b31e0e64f3773ddd2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4551ce1e7653dda2331ab8ba0712bbf7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-930256b3bb4008d11b0886f29973ea67&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9588cab79f107d9108d8259469c3e906&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5169c21d505e8c064ac1ba9c16c3eb50&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-acfa11b893b09fc1fb7d6655be073aa3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-86420cc97941914b0b1ab8dfd5d58107&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0b4cc500f0cf6ffd63a8f594c9055457&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-64e2adfc473177ee34daf0edf44e9fab</data>
