    <node id="Fences">
      <data key="d0">Fences</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Fences are structures designed as barriers for various purposes, primarily to promote safety, enhance aesthetics, and limit negative impacts on the environment and visibility. They serve crucial roles in residential settings, providing privacy and security while contributing to the overall appearance of properties. Fences can be constructed from various materials and may vary in height based on specific regulatory standards.

Fencing regulations dictate the allowable height, style, placement, and materials for fences within residential zones. These regulations include guidelines specifically for corner lots and setbacks, ensuring that fences do not obstruct visibility or compromise safety. The standards vary by location, with Title 33 regulations outlining the installation and maintenance requirements for fences around properties.

Importantly, fences are regulated within zoning codes, which define the acceptable parameters for their construction. This ensures that fences are appropriately designed to balance individual property needs with community standards, contributing to a harmonious neighborhood environment. Overall, fences play a significant role in defining property boundaries while adhering to local regulations that help maintain neighborhood safety and aesthetics.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-40b451e83bd236d5610bd2be4f5e2e51&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fd49cb12bde2e181e43e4b3ea44c114c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5348591467a41c77d858f5dbd717c172&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-72db2074515977339664a4c6fb188af1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-495efc01559ae21a68e9da3b57998769&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0ce4a0b57a87c710f0ef76d5570894bf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4271bb9cc0287dcb0b401b760eb4864e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f9bdd0a72bd1cfe3ef9d36a7ad4b1bbb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b2003f93c2ff07e93de1875ffe1520e0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cec6039135156cca92456b8fc09b0f8c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-54018cb95b587cdb265182d453408b50</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1768781078</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Title 32">
      <data key="d0">Title 32</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Title 32 refers to a specific section of the Portland City Code that encompasses regulations related to signs and their placement within the jurisdiction. It includes comprehensive guidelines governing signage, touching upon various aspects such as sign standards, approval criteria, and associated requirements pertinent to urban planning and zoning.

Specifically, Title 32 addresses regulations concerning signs, which are critical for ensuring compliance with zoning and development standards. It sets forth the criteria necessary for the approval of signs, detailing requirements that must be met in various planning contexts. The regulations outlined in Title 32 aim to foster aesthetic considerations and safety by establishing uniform standards for signage.

Moreover, Title 32 plays a significant role in regulating the placement of signs within designated areas, thus contributing to effective urban planning and development. Its provisions are vital for maintaining order and coherence in the built environment, ensuring that signs serve their intended purpose without compromising the visual integrity of neighborhoods. Overall, Title 32 is essential for managing signage regulations that facilitate both functionality and community standards in Portland.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7b7c5b32a510f170e1363780c7ccaa64&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c84bdf4402c70c4d4ae69283e01b9ab4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5348591467a41c77d858f5dbd717c172&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b430ca141ad0936b7b20ef5f23780667&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-40e6e0a14b42f25e67a43e11831d8486&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b0460f02d0e013f6aa12de53db834a01&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-250f22c8b2433134e7522ccaf4bb55d8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-02eec69c467b67bcbd30527c47bb489f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4e75ce8cc1ed34d85d9e574add4504b6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-225b86068366c52c4915a3742fa8cb4a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dd435149dc3e05a98f074fcc3dd3ac12&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3e735d7bf40b332127efe9da20dd2f85</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
