    <node id="Detached Covered Accessory Structures">
      <data key="d0">Detached Covered Accessory Structures</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Detached covered accessory structures refer to additional buildings connected or separate from a primary structure, subject to specific coverage standards.&lt;SEP&gt;Detached covered accessory structures are secondary buildings on a property, subject to various height and setback regulations.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-97c549f439b335c735e299563e1e4c63&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9a48525044c3c1bf75799ef0954937b7</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1768781079</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Primary Structure">
      <data key="d0">Primary Structure</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">A primary structure is the main building on a property, with standards set for accessories and their relation to it.&lt;SEP&gt;The Primary Structure is the main building against which the design standards for the Detached Covered Accessory Structure are compared.&lt;SEP&gt;The primary structure refers to the main building on a property, which has specific regulations governing accessory structures built on the same lot.&lt;SEP&gt;A primary structure is the main building on a property, which has distinct standards for alterations.&lt;SEP&gt;The primary structure refers to the main building that is subject to the regulations for alterations, facades, and outdoor elements.&lt;SEP&gt;A Primary Structure is a chief structure on a site, primarily used for the main activities of that site as defined by size and placement.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-97c549f439b335c735e299563e1e4c63&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b77775bfe50a48aa700ab96a9ab1c440&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9a48525044c3c1bf75799ef0954937b7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9cfe18720ad9b5bf5dadab610a40b064&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-49385b6cdb2ddda6bb336746708e24c5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c7643c0f4d3b8e865b2421a8cf1882d9</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1768781079</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="F2 Standards">
      <data key="d0">F2 Standards</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">F2 standards are regulations outlined in Chapter 33.248 concerning landscaping and screening requirements.&lt;SEP&gt;The F2 standards are regulatory benchmarks outlined in Chapter 33.248, focusing on landscaping and screening requirements in developments.&lt;SEP&gt;F2 standards are part of the landscaping and screening regulations outlined in Chapter 33.248.&lt;SEP&gt;F2 Standards refers to specific fencing requirements applicable to certain types of developments.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-97c549f439b335c735e299563e1e4c63&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9a48525044c3c1bf75799ef0954937b7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-deccf49edc8456f9e632a30209a8e62a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4328dc69e2d2b9998d2666c8193b0245</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1768781079</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Exterior Finish Materials">
      <data key="d0">Exterior Finish Materials</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Exterior finish materials refer to the requirements for the outer surface of detached covered accessory structures, ensuring aesthetic compatibility with primary structures.&lt;SEP&gt;Exterior finish materials describe the acceptable and prohibited materials used for building facades, ensuring design quality.&lt;SEP&gt;Exterior Finish Materials include specific allowable materials for the outer surfaces of buildings, detailing which materials cannot be used.&lt;SEP&gt;Exterior finish materials are materials used to cover the outer surface of a building, subject to specific standards and regulations.&lt;SEP&gt;Exterior finish materials are the materials used for the facades of buildings, subject to certain standards and regulations.&lt;SEP&gt;Exterior Finish Materials are the materials used on building facades that must meet specific aesthetic standards.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-97c549f439b335c735e299563e1e4c63&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b1abf9c5e7468a8aa27d8da7b65c0352&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a6dbd7e7cb1c5715db55af9b8b09b073&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1e3fdad639580e09c9504162fba29af8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d37ff07ffa7013303eef3ff22ca4c664&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-49385b6cdb2ddda6bb336746708e24c5</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1768781079</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Roof Pitch">
      <data key="d0">Roof Pitch</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Roof pitch refers to the steepness of a roof, which must adhere to specific regulations for detached covered accessory structures.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-97c549f439b335c735e299563e1e4c63</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1768781079</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Trim">
      <data key="d0">Trim</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Trim pertains to the decorative elements on a detached covered accessory structure that must meet specified requirements to align with the primary structure.&lt;SEP&gt;Trim refers to decorative finishes applied to building elements such as roofs, porches, windows, and doors, with specifications on width.&lt;SEP&gt;Trim is specified as a necessary element to mark roof lines, windows, and doors on all building elevations.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-97c549f439b335c735e299563e1e4c63&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b1abf9c5e7468a8aa27d8da7b65c0352&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d6b729b503408753bfb450541d299ca7</data>
