    <node id="Public Right-of-Way">
      <data key="d0">Public Right-of-Way</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Public Right-of-Way refers to dedicated access ways that influence building setback requirements for adjacent structures.&lt;SEP&gt;Public Right-of-Way refers to publicly accessible areas that may influence the designation of primary versus accessory uses in zoning regulations.&lt;SEP&gt;Public Right-of-Way refers to the areas designated for public use, including streets and sidewalks, where certain improvements are regulated.&lt;SEP&gt;Public right-of-way refers to land set aside for public use, often for transportation or utilities, where stormwater management facilities can be located.&lt;SEP&gt;The public right-of-way refers to land dedicated for public use and access, which affects where notices can be posted.&lt;SEP&gt;Public right-of-way refers to areas designated for public use which may be influenced by the institution's development.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f92d57fd526433c19e4bdb08e2032fe0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-be5907995aac72aa1fae17ac5227927a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7623c76eb73dcf7a97d644a7c57616d7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8e988f81291359d27584c02f5af917de&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5b1548cc5d56ac0b8d2ab93d83d89c89&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cd3ca6674563552f618f0433e3c4016a</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
