    <node id="Pedestrian District">
      <data key="d0">Pedestrian District</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The Pedestrian District is a designated area specifically developed to prioritize pedestrian traffic and accessibility within urban environments. This concept involves implementing specific standards and regulations that influence the design, placement, and layout of buildings, sidewalks, and accessways to foster a pedestrian-friendly atmosphere.

Pedestrian Districts focus on encouraging walking by establishing guidelines that affect building setbacks and height. These regulations are intended to enhance the street-level experience and promote non-residential uses, stimulating economic activity and social engagement in these areas. By prioritizing foot traffic, these districts contribute to improved urban mobility and connectivity.

Within specific urban planning frameworks, such as the East Corridor Plan District, Pedestrian Districts are designed with a clear emphasis on increasing pedestrian activity. They are characterized by a set of zoning standards that facilitate more accessible and appealing environments for walking. Overall, the Pedestrian District embodies a holistic approach to urban design, enhancing both pedestrian movement and the overall livability of the area.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6e17537bd24336b2dd65681e37f4d9aa&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-07f772c2b474f898ac2fe23797d82b3d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cbb810e875d66f848b351a4f1271e2a7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7dce4a7be57273a9865cad046f69ab21&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d735bdaad55969341cee3cca65a09caf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b5e32407319712c8e952f9496e61eac1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c05d0565960f8c21b2582374b4856a38&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1e3fdad639580e09c9504162fba29af8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e8a638cacad1df6f7e7b142a2867c220&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b1c01837a01a91075eb01c907ccb32c8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8a043b4f466e82f380e009054fb98e04</data>
