    <node id="E">
      <data key="d0">E</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">E is a zoning designation that signifies employment-oriented areas within urban planning.&lt;SEP&gt;E is a zoning designation typically associated with employment and industrial areas.&lt;SEP&gt;E stands for Employment zoning category used in planning and zoning regulations.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-bb053937b4fd6958f885edbd491a92e1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-03672adab686e2724fe4ae10d50293ed&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-54d3c1a461749c2773607b44c831a1a7</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1768781085</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Inclusionary Housing">
      <data key="d0">Inclusionary Housing</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Inclusionary Housing refers to a set of policies and regulations designed to promote affordable housing within new developments. At its core, Inclusionary Housing requires or incentivizes developers to integrate a certain percentage of affordable housing units into their projects, making it essential for accommodating low- or moderate-income residents in urban settings.

The framework for Inclusionary Housing also includes zoning policies and regulations that can provide developers with incentives, such as increased height limits and Floor Area Ratio (FAR) bonuses for projects that fulfill specific affordable housing criteria. These incentives encourage developers to contribute to the affordable housing stock while maintaining the financial viability of their projects.

Inclusionary Housing is governed by specific regulations, such as those outlined in Chapter 33.245, which establishes the requirements and eligibility criteria for developments that trigger these affordable housing provisions. Overall, Inclusionary Housing aims to create a balanced and equitable housing market by ensuring that affordable units are a consistent part of new residential developments.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-586262724750ed4bbb098ac0aed41049&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c8c8009e101b1c5acef3b9262a76129e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dd5e24f7c98b2b609a3ed6656b608420&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-17a8112955d64121a20078f6914b8018&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dae5a6d220ddce8db9baaa910a116b0b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f8dedd6570aca440bb011dbffebca8f0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d93214ee5c49fbb5384175df60c9eafe&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b9e2c7926ceca17c4d0d7614d1c8acec&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b2d0e3bbe363516aa161846014338967&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b2003f93c2ff07e93de1875ffe1520e0</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1768781085</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Ord. No. 190477">
      <data key="d0">Ord. No. 190477</data>
      <data key="d1">other</data>
      <data key="d2">Ord. No. 190477 is a legislative ordinance that became effective on August 1, 2021. It serves as an amendment to regulations pertaining to planning and zoning, specifically impacting areas such as the Portland Citywide Design Guidelines and the Central City Plan District. The ordinance is recognized as the most recent amendment to these standards and encompasses various aspects of local regulations.

The measure was specifically designed to amend Title 33, which includes several provisions affecting land use reviews and design review policies within designated districts. The repeated emphasis on the effective date highlights its importance in establishing a new regulatory framework as of August 1, 2021.

In summary, Ord. No. 190477 is an important piece of legislation that updates and amends existing regulations governing planning and zoning in the affected areas, facilitating improved design review and land use practices throughout the jurisdiction.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ca3ac296609c32cc7de194b33256a6fa&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cdfe21baa0f0ca21d6a17ac611149488&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-92185d9fe6c20f7172332a56db23c66f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ac7f584fe857b5fce6394c52a4277eee&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d832d62a221ee980072fc5fd0b98677e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a23f6f27b8a490522d8ea471d51807cb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e1998ebd13056e67974311eecd3d3c24&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e6571cdc55e7ba51879f95332dd75ffd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8df3a6e155b7b2dbe62a2cf5f8c6d3b3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6d3f9ffb1d8c7f041a89cac2f7df9d56&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-07f525a8329ffe0f200c2898df08550d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-11f829729d0020581f29820d44e30308&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dbde9b26d1da0b6d201fb666deeea1af&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a103c5d3dba321e0474c52d261504cde&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-781cca6130ae27c4d81ffe1ab1fb3d43&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1bce80dce3f6e46fbf15b845bbded504&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-36f0a7e50ec5c262cdb4140ddc318c8f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7fce67d5a707ef7b953fc44e3ab5449b</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1768781085</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="I">
      <data key="d0">I</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">I represents industrial zoning categories intended for manufacturing and logistics activities.&lt;SEP&gt;I denotes Industrial zoning category as defined in planning and zoning regulations.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-bb053937b4fd6958f885edbd491a92e1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-03672adab686e2724fe4ae10d50293ed</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
