    <node id="Windows">
      <data key="d0">Windows</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Windows refer to the structural openings in the facades of buildings that serve a crucial role in allowing light and air into enclosed spaces. These openings must adhere to specific design standards that regulate their size, placement, and alignment with the overall architectural style of the structure. The requirements for windows ensure that they contribute effectively to a building's facade area, particularly in street-facing aspects where they help maintain visual connections to the street and enhance public safety.

Windows on detached covered accessory structures are also subject to regulations to ensure their style and alignment are consistent with that of the primary structure. In urban planning and architectural design, the inclusion of windows is essential not only for aesthetic considerations but also for meeting building standards related to visibility and safety.

Furthermore, windows can be replaced under specific guidelines that address material use and installation standards, especially in designated historic districts. In such instances, materials like wood or metal-clad wood may be favored to preserve the building's historical character. The regulations include provisions for how replacement windows should maintain the integrity and visibility of the structure, ensuring that both contemporary and historic design principles are respected. 

Overall, the significance of windows extends beyond their functional purpose; they are integral to both the aesthetic and regulatory fabric of building design, serving as essential architectural elements that must meet a variety of standards and guidelines.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-97c549f439b335c735e299563e1e4c63&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-06ffebbe5f5a48ace7018e8814873e24&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-abc8929a3f5f26daf160f30332a927b6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3b3d78b716894f51a8b9d0830f8f790e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3722780cf7d6963b782ca94662383bba&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0ce4a0b57a87c710f0ef76d5570894bf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b1abf9c5e7468a8aa27d8da7b65c0352&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a6dbd7e7cb1c5715db55af9b8b09b073&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4ab8e6f4ae4a675657fc86e7ca06a385&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d6b729b503408753bfb450541d299ca7</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1768781090</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Buffer Overlay Zone">
      <data key="d0">Buffer Overlay Zone</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The Buffer Overlay Zone refers to areas where specific regulations apply to setbacks and structures to maintain spacing from streets and other uses.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a19a2393681785537b2d0f83af94a363</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1768781090</data>
