    <node id="Public Art">
      <data key="d0">Public Art</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Public Art is defined as artistic works placed in public spaces, serving various functions and purposes within urban environments. This includes artworks that may fulfill specific zoning requirements, particularly for window coverage in commercial or mixed-use buildings, as well as campus institutional zones. Public Art can serve as a substitute for ground-floor windows in E Zones, thereby contributing to compliance with local zoning regulations.

The installation and display of Public Art are subject to local definitions and regulations, often requiring approval from the relevant authorities, such as property owners and the City Arts Program. This oversight helps ensure that the artworks enhance community engagement and offer aesthetic value to the areas they occupy. Public Art is further elaborated upon in Chapter 5.74, which outlines the criteria and guidelines governing these installations, noting that they can also be considered during alterations that do not necessitate a historic resource review.

Through its unique integration into public spaces, Public Art plays a significant role in shaping the cultural landscape of communities, making art accessible to the public while meeting regulatory standards.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e1ee11d492136a3cb6116df2011b3bcd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-62277f559200167b441652bb61e8226f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-08792af032c7577571c2943db7371a28&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c1643cca9962c7d0ac7fc6f08d56ed54&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-79f19e6435928fe4a325e8a1a4cc5dd1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a2020f8cb9e1e9fff0f2699c2142d64b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a7282e754cd453819f3e67c09c8e7a91&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-639e5332d0e90ed7ad51bb327c530214</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
