    <node id="Facade">
      <data key="d0">Facade</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">A facade is the exterior face of a building, particularly the front, which must meet specific standards for materials such as glazing or glass block.&lt;SEP&gt;Facade refers to the front or face of a building, particularly street-facing ones, which must adhere to specific design standards such as window requirements.&lt;SEP&gt;Facade is defined as the outer face or wall of a structure, viewed from one side, particularly relevant in architectural measurements.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-cbb810e875d66f848b351a4f1271e2a7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-219169bf18bf9f73b3370be8bd44ed1d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4ab8e6f4ae4a675657fc86e7ca06a385</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1768781095</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Residential Use">
      <data key="d0">Residential Use</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Residential use indicates that a property is occupied for dwelling purposes, influencing zoning regulations and building standards.&lt;SEP&gt;Residential use refers to living spaces that are exempt from certain ground floor use regulations within the Centers Main Street Overlay Zone until January 1, 2029.&lt;SEP&gt;Residential Use refers to properties allocated for living accommodations, which are temporarily exempt from certain ground floor active use standards until 2029.&lt;SEP&gt;Residential use refers to the classification of floor areas designated for housing and living purposes within zoning regulations.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-cbb810e875d66f848b351a4f1271e2a7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5f1c1bab5c230ae2f2d1c810ec319973&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0da7d185f05e10a8fa434d3d4713ae5d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3b4c57adc2cf94d9a0a99114828453df</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1768781095</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Street Setback">
      <data key="d0">Street Setback</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Street setback defines the distance from a street where no structures may be built, maintaining space for sidewalks and visual corridors.&lt;SEP&gt;Street setback refers to the distance required from the street to the edge of structures, influencing zoning regulations.&lt;SEP&gt;Street setback is measured from a street lot line.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-cbb810e875d66f848b351a4f1271e2a7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1ad3df252c10f7d3d9869375a3dd70b5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4e75ce8cc1ed34d85d9e574add4504b6</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1768781095</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Civic Corridors">
      <data key="d0">Civic Corridors</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Civic corridors are designated areas that emphasize pedestrian traffic and community interaction, affecting setback regulations.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-cbb810e875d66f848b351a4f1271e2a7</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1768781095</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
