    <node id="Bureau of Transportation">
      <data key="d0">Bureau of Transportation</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">The Bureau of Transportation is a governmental agency tasked with a wide range of responsibilities related to transportation planning and regulation within a city. It plays a crucial role in approving the design and alignment of pedestrian connections and overseeing regulations pertaining to both pedestrian and street connections. Additionally, the Bureau manages transportation-related activities, including the administration of the Bicycle Parking Fund and related improvements, thereby promoting sustainable transportation options within the urban environment.

Further, the Bureau of Transportation advises the City Council on various transportation matters, including traffic aspects of the Comprehensive Transportation Management Plan and the Good Neighbor Agreement. This advisory role extends to reviewing transportation impacts during the land use review process and potentially requiring Transportation Impact Studies for land use applications concerning transportation systems. The agency is also involved in the preliminary approval of proposed streets and is the authority to which operational reports regarding parking must be submitted upon request.

In terms of specific districts, the Bureau of Transportation actively manages transportation planning and services in areas like the West Portland Multicultural Plan District and evaluates transportation-related proposals to ensure compliance with regulations, especially in the Columbia South Shore Plan District. The Bureau oversees parking regulations and applications in Portland, further emphasizing its comprehensive role in transportation demand management and compliance with associated guidelines across the city.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9b26f7d2f189f75baf65a70e0e0ddf9a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ac9c74456e021e6800b45f3d57ae1aea&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-083abb85def98a799fa4d4f0c3d8ab61&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-023787323ecc1bdd6b648be267e3ae8d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dae5a6d220ddce8db9baaa910a116b0b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c78b8d8f5df1d607fc21562bc53c145e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-614d099bcb8d71cfc787e0cbccc1ebbf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-feb900a5dfbcef784bb89e24f0c85c97&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4234265dfa77d91d264b8fa460dd2ae7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-256d1ee4f0b33311aca7f325efe86843&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-760f8bc033fa383332ea7e6ae26dff29&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b0460f02d0e013f6aa12de53db834a01&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a5f5ada1bd73ae6d3ab8a5bb43ff6e2b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-afbed3d0d9f1ce8f7b4b9d7df85935cb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-550081f2dd423e67b6d83d9a91ecd5c3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f9daedca8ce38c66f76f89f5342070ce&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c4ea648c5114e40203cea6e5e6e2bb27</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1768781099</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Floor Area Ratio (FAR)">
      <data key="d0">Floor Area Ratio (FAR)</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Floor Area Ratio (FAR) is a measure used to determine the maximum allowable building area relative to the size of the lot.&lt;SEP&gt;Floor Area Ratio (FAR) regulates the intensity of land use and is expressed as a ratio comparing the building floor area to the land area in a subdistrict.&lt;SEP&gt;Floor Area Ratio refers to the ratio of a building's total floor area to the size of the piece of land upon which it is built, affecting density regulations.&lt;SEP&gt;Floor Area Ratio (FAR) is a measurement of the relationship between the amount of floor area and site area, expressed in square feet.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c815f0219b3fa08de12163f428266e23&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d06cb95ab17fc909ffe773dee0f0a731&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f6f3910d4b4ea8a4a76516cfd107b355&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-133e0d785413e64759688100094b7c2e</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1768781099</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
