    <node id="Environmental Resource Tracts">
      <data key="d0">Environmental Resource Tracts</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Environmental resource tracts are areas that must be owned in common by landowners and are protected from development activities.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-aacf586128716bb40a914b64e071f666</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1768781100</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Homeowners Association">
      <data key="d0">Homeowners Association</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">A homeowner’s association is an organization of homeowners that manages common areas and enforces community rules.&lt;SEP&gt;A Homeowners’ Association is an organization comprising members of a community to manage shared elements and enforce rules for land divisions.&lt;SEP&gt;The Homeowners’ Association oversees the maintenance and ownership of shared spaces and rights-of-way within residential developments.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-aacf586128716bb40a914b64e071f666&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-09653fd8cb264cda4f3441bd478fe017&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-22acd36bdbe56e46354926a0904b7463</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1768781100</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Public Agency">
      <data key="d0">Public Agency</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">A public agency is a government entity that may own or manage land within environmental resource tracts.&lt;SEP&gt;A public agency operates services such as shelters, counseling, and education for the community.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-aacf586128716bb40a914b64e071f666&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9588cab79f107d9108d8259469c3e906</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1768781100</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Non-Profit Organization">
      <data key="d0">Non-Profit Organization</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">A non-profit organization may also own or manage tracts dedicated to environmental resources.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-aacf586128716bb40a914b64e071f666</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1768781100</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Table 475-5">
      <data key="d0">Table 475-5</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">Table 475-5 provides maximum disturbance area allowances for various land division zones within the River Environmental overlay zone.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-aacf586128716bb40a914b64e071f666</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1768781100</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Table 475-6">
      <data key="d0">Table 475-6</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">Table 475-6 outlines maximum roadway and right-of-way widths for different base zone types.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-aacf586128716bb40a914b64e071f666</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1768781100</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Property Line Adjustments (PLAs)">
      <data key="d0">Property Line Adjustments (PLAs)</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Property Line Adjustments refer to the process of relocating property lines while adhering to specific standards in the River Environmental overlay zone.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-aacf586128716bb40a914b64e071f666</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1768781100</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="50 Percent">
      <data key="d0">50 Percent</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The term 50 percent refers to the minimum area size required to meet specified standards for development and improvement.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-aacf586128716bb40a914b64e071f666</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1768781100</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="100 Square Feet">
      <data key="d0">100 Square Feet</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">100 square feet is the threshold below which simplified mitigation area requirements apply in development standards.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-aacf586128716bb40a914b64e071f666</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1768781100</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="50 Square Feet">
      <data key="d0">50 Square Feet</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">50 Square Feet is the minimum area used in calculations for planting fees and requirements in the revegetation projects.&lt;SEP&gt;50 square feet is the minimum mitigation area required for proposed developments less than 100 square feet.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-aacf586128716bb40a914b64e071f666&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3059e173ef884a58e8cd223998e6158d</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1768781100</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Utility Construction">
      <data key="d0">Utility Construction</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Utility construction refers to the activities related to the building and installation of utility lines and systems, which are subject to specific standards.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-aacf586128716bb40a914b64e071f666</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1768781100</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Streets, Alleys, Walkways, and Stormwater Facilities">
      <data key="d0">Streets, Alleys, Walkways, and Stormwater Facilities</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Streets, alleys, walkways, and stormwater facilities are infrastructure elements regulated to ensure they are not located near wetlands or water bodies.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-aacf586128716bb40a914b64e071f666</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1768781100</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Right of Way">
      <data key="d0">Right of Way</data>
      <data key="d1">location</data>
      <data key="d2">Right of Way denotes a designated area or easement that allows for passage and accessibility to properties.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-32085ee92ea490a135a4678599552bbc</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1768781100</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Urban Green Features Standard">
      <data key="d0">Urban Green Features Standard</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Urban Green Features Standard outlines requirements for integrating green elements in urban developments, especially in the West Portland Multicultural Plan District.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-32085ee92ea490a135a4678599552bbc</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1768781100</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Energy Star">
      <data key="d0">Energy Star</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Energy Star is a program that certifies products and building designs for energy efficiency, particularly in solar reflectance.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-32085ee92ea490a135a4678599552bbc</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1768781100</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Large Tree Species">
      <data key="d0">Large Tree Species</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Large Tree Species refers to specific types of trees that meet certain criteria established in landscaping and environmental standards.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-32085ee92ea490a135a4678599552bbc</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1768781100</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Ecoroof">
      <data key="d0">Ecoroof</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">An Ecoroof is an environmentally-friendly landscaping option that contributes toward landscaping area standards by substituting ecoroof space.&lt;SEP&gt;The Ecoroof is a sustainable roofing option that contributes to the required landscaped area at a specific area ratio.&lt;SEP&gt;An Ecoroof is a green roofing system that must cover a certain percentage of the total roof area of new buildings.&lt;SEP&gt;An ecoroof is a vegetated roof area adjacent to buildings that impacts the bird-safe exterior glazing standards.&lt;SEP&gt;An Ecoroof is a green roofing system that incorporates vegetation to improve insulation and mitigate stormwater runoff, as specified in urban planning standards.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-32085ee92ea490a135a4678599552bbc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0c749708919a67aa6d2facb52ce88c3d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c2e596f70f1bcab1802ff190bf7807a1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-160a32f5ce16b3d3271378c2f5cf5dac&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-91db399b32e598af5683382ecc53fa6a</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1768781100</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Paragraph 33.610.200.D.1">
      <data key="d0">Paragraph 33.610.200.D.1</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Paragraph 33.610.200.D.1 outlines the minimum lot width standard for land division.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-59fe4540439f409de13002eb40172d2d</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1768781100</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Multi-Dwelling and IR Zones">
      <data key="d0">Multi-Dwelling and IR Zones</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Multi-Dwelling and IR Zones are zoning classifications that have distinct standards for density and lot dimensions.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-59fe4540439f409de13002eb40172d2d</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1768781101</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Detached Covered Accessory Structure">
      <data key="d0">Detached Covered Accessory Structure</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The Detached Covered Accessory Structure refers to a type of building that must meet specific design standards regarding windows and eaves.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b77775bfe50a48aa700ab96a9ab1c440</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1768781101</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Vertical Structures">
      <data key="d0">Vertical Structures</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Vertical Structures encompass items such as flag poles, trellises, and play structures, each subject to specific height and setback standards.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b77775bfe50a48aa700ab96a9ab1c440</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1768781101</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Height Regulations">
      <data key="d0">Height Regulations</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Height Regulations specify the maximum allowable elevation for various structures, including detached uncovered vertical structures.&lt;SEP&gt;Height regulations specify the maximum allowable height for structures, including fences, in certain zones.&lt;SEP&gt;Height Regulations define how the height of structures is measured and the limits that apply in designated subdistricts to protect views.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b77775bfe50a48aa700ab96a9ab1c440&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4271bb9cc0287dcb0b401b760eb4864e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-78755af7dcf0cbd31bb8a59f246d601c</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1768781101</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Detached Uncovered Decks">
      <data key="d0">Detached Uncovered Decks</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Detached Uncovered Decks are a type of structure that must adhere to specific regulations regarding height and setbacks, similar to other detached structures.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b77775bfe50a48aa700ab96a9ab1c440</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1768781101</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Detached Uncovered Ramps">
      <data key="d0">Detached Uncovered Ramps</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Detached Uncovered Ramps refer to pathway structures that must meet specific height and setback regulations in building codes.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b77775bfe50a48aa700ab96a9ab1c440</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1768781101</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Detached Uncovered Stairways">
      <data key="d0">Detached Uncovered Stairways</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Detached Uncovered Stairways are structures that provide access and must comply with height and setback requirements according to the building regulations.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b77775bfe50a48aa700ab96a9ab1c440</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1768781101</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Ground Lease">
      <data key="d0">Ground Lease</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The ground lease is a legal document that outlines the terms under which land is leased, must have a year remaining, and details the boundaries of the lease.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-608046d7256ca4fab03fcfe5e6c5d66b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-05ab6bd54c89f86d004823d37a33255e</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1768781101</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Utility Easement">
      <data key="d0">Utility Easement</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The utility easement is a legal agreement allowing utility companies to access land for utilities, also requiring detailed boundaries and documentation.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-608046d7256ca4fab03fcfe5e6c5d66b</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1768781101</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Building Permit Application">
      <data key="d0">Building Permit Application</data>
      <data key="d1">document</data>
      <data key="d2">The building permit application is required to show the boundaries of the ground lease or utility easement on a site plan.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-608046d7256ca4fab03fcfe5e6c5d66b</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1768781101</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Table 258-1">
      <data key="d0">Table 258-1</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">Table 258-1 outlines the compliance periods necessary for bringing sites into compliance with regulations.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-608046d7256ca4fab03fcfe5e6c5d66b</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1768781101</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Grace Period">
      <data key="d0">Grace Period</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The grace period is a two-year timeframe starting after compliance certification during which no upgrades to nonconforming development are required.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-608046d7256ca4fab03fcfe5e6c5d66b</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1768781101</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Mitigation Site">
      <data key="d0">Mitigation Site</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">A Mitigation Site is a property designated for implementing mitigation measures to offset the impacts of development on resources and functional values.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-2fec75523a13dc705f1116777ea0ce94</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1768781101</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Legal Instrument">
      <data key="d0">Legal Instrument</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">A Legal Instrument, such as an easement or deed restriction, is a document that grants the applicant authority over a Mitigation Site for successful program execution.&lt;SEP&gt;Legal Instrument refers to documents such as easements or deed restrictions that are necessary for carrying out the mitigation program.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-2fec75523a13dc705f1116777ea0ce94&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2001ba277158737c9b888248e680d7ee</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1768781101</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Table 475-7">
      <data key="d0">Table 475-7</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">Table 475-7 outlines the minimum area required outside of the River Environmental Overlay Zone for various zoning classifications.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f5a5816615430d9b588c4adaf4aa3131</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1768781101</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Option One">
      <data key="d0">Option One</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Option One is a process for correcting violations involving the removal of illegal development and requires replanting and repair of any damage.&lt;SEP&gt;Option One is a correction method for environmental violations that involves removal of illegal development and replanting.&lt;SEP&gt;Option One refers to the first process for handling violations, involving removal and repair of illegal developments.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f5a5816615430d9b588c4adaf4aa3131&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2b95c123bc42f5e9142018f60381ac37&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2c77c0e594bc319fd143848146f9ea56</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1768781101</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="PIR Master Plan">
      <data key="d0">PIR Master Plan</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The PIR Master Plan is a comprehensive development strategy specifically designed for the Portland International Raceway and its surrounding Plan District. This plan is a critical document that outlines a variety of elements and procedures necessary for the responsible and effective development of the area.

Key aspects of the PIR Master Plan include its emphasis on environmental, operational, and transportation considerations that must be taken into account for proposed developments. It serves as a guiding framework aimed at managing development and traffic impacts, ensuring that these factors are thoughtfully addressed throughout the planning process.

The plan also delineates development goals along with essential components tailored to the specific site, promoting community participation in shaping future developments. It establishes clear criteria and requirements for planning and development activities within the designated district, thereby providing developers with guidelines and standards that govern acceptable practices and objectives.

Moreover, the PIR Master Plan addresses practical operational concerns such as stormwater management and noise control measures, ensuring that new developments align with both community needs and environmental responsibilities. Through these various elements, the PIR Master Plan functions as a strategic framework that not only fosters orderly growth but also enhances the overall functionality of the Portland International Raceway area.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9e362e6c3818ce32276e136b8d6e3bbc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3a0531b6f064b40b2004eb7208dfda5c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-df882fbc3ea7a9dfe92da1c882f4bac6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1cd2a226a2530256c685d499d90fe0b4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a86ce100d88532ab8ca2f1d785c9a636&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-df361d0b769c8729ae58a7e716789b6e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8c25a4b5dcdfc23b3bd64f8c6d4bbc00&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eb3edee7f4a38fc6771990a8a0505744</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1768781101</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Environmental Regulations">
      <data key="d0">Environmental Regulations</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Environmental Regulations encompass the standards and practices aimed at preserving natural resources within designated zones.&lt;SEP&gt;Environmental regulations are the rules governing the protection of significant natural resources in the Columbia South Shore Plan District.&lt;SEP&gt;Environmental Regulations are rules applied to land use in the Johnson Creek Basin to minimize impacts on natural resources.&lt;SEP&gt;Environmental Regulations refer to the rules set to safeguard the environment in areas like the Johnson Creek Basin Plan District.&lt;SEP&gt;Environmental regulations refer to the rules and standards governing activities that impact ecological zones, particularly in relation to the airport district.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f5a5816615430d9b588c4adaf4aa3131&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-490cb02a9aa37ab5ed0231106009cb20&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-903c333e61f6db5523241ff9b270a90f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-11f829729d0020581f29820d44e30308&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d4d54757bbd8eb99b2b0cd39a957603d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1e8e044b1fd94ca567e1458bbd0323ca&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-df192280c989d3e1d2bc59bf520124ed</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1768781101</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Willamette">
      <data key="d0">Willamette</data>
      <data key="d1">location</data>
      <data key="d2">The Willamette is a river that the text references for its banks and regulations regarding riverbank development.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3ec46b8cc5650199d033fa9573b44034</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1768781101</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Riverbank Mitigation Plan">
      <data key="d0">Riverbank Mitigation Plan</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The Riverbank Mitigation Plan outlines strategies to improve the appearance and ecological health of the riverbank as part of development activities.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3ec46b8cc5650199d033fa9573b44034</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1768781101</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Landslide Hazard Study">
      <data key="d0">Landslide Hazard Study</data>
      <data key="d1">document</data>
      <data key="d2">The Landslide Hazard Study is a requirement for evaluation of development proposals within landslide hazard areas to assess risk and appropriate measures.&lt;SEP&gt;The Landslide Hazard Study is an evaluation tool used to assess the risk of landslides and inform development plans.&lt;SEP&gt;The Landslide Hazard Study includes an evaluation of landslide hazards on a proposed development site, assessing risk and recommending design strategies for mitigation.&lt;SEP&gt;The Landslide Hazard Study is an evaluation meant to assess risks associated with landslides for proposed building locations.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-41e19a3ba631255b8cad74289a68c75f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7c35c570b66abd053c37dd3812df90d3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f65e5459208816c049f71f8dc6ffc84e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b68e1edc738fe00a1738dc12a945ce96</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1768781101</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="33.633 Phased Land Divisions and Staged Final Plats">
      <data key="d0">33.633 Phased Land Divisions and Staged Final Plats</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Section 33.633 details the regulations for phased land divisions and staging of final plats in city planning.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-41e19a3ba631255b8cad74289a68c75f</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1768781101</data>
