    <node id="Bureau of Environmental Services (BES)">
      <data key="d0">Bureau of Environmental Services (BES)</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">The Bureau of Environmental Services (BES) is an agency that provides oversight for stormwater management and ecological practices in the specified area.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6fe16dacd4c0cb29ef445c2340ee2812</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1768781109</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Transportation Impact Study">
      <data key="d0">Transportation Impact Study</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">The transportation impact study is an assessment process used to evaluate the consequences of land development on traffic and transportation systems.&lt;SEP&gt;The Transportation Impact Study assesses the effects of proposed developments on local transportation systems and traffic patterns.&lt;SEP&gt;The Transportation Impact Study is a required analysis for certain land divisions based on proposed development impacts.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6fe16dacd4c0cb29ef445c2340ee2812&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f65e5459208816c049f71f8dc6ffc84e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fc617f6f881f3f98f97136cfa84f2ea2</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1768781109</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Chapter 33.641">
      <data key="d0">Chapter 33.641</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Chapter 33.641 is a section of the Portland City Code that addresses transportation impacts and related standards in land use planning.&lt;SEP&gt;Chapter 33.641 outlines regulations concerning transportation impacts in land divisions.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6fe16dacd4c0cb29ef445c2340ee2812&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f464bb6caf4196f9f098e5debb9f7fc4</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1768781109</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="River-Dependent">
      <data key="d0">River-Dependent</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">River-Dependent refers to uses that can only be conducted on or near a river, necessitating access for activities like transportation and recreation.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a7aa2e9895420bcb5392c9333db8bab4</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1768781109</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="River-Related">
      <data key="d0">River-Related</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">River-Related denotes uses or developments not directly dependent on access to water bodies but associated with river-related activities.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a7aa2e9895420bcb5392c9333db8bab4</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1768781109</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Scenic Site">
      <data key="d0">Scenic Site</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">A Scenic Site is an area valued for its aesthetic characteristics, often featuring natural or landscaped elements.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a7aa2e9895420bcb5392c9333db8bab4</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1768781109</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Scenic View">
      <data key="d0">Scenic View</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">A Scenic View is a vantage point offering a framed or panoramic view that may include natural or manmade features.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a7aa2e9895420bcb5392c9333db8bab4</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1768781109</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Scenic Viewpoint">
      <data key="d0">Scenic Viewpoint</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">A Scenic Viewpoint is a location specifically designated for enjoying a scenic view, which may have multiple vantage points.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a7aa2e9895420bcb5392c9333db8bab4</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1768781109</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Seep or Spring">
      <data key="d0">Seep or Spring</data>
      <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
      <data key="d2">A Seep or Spring is an area where groundwater emerges on the land surface, creating saturated conditions or visible flow.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a7aa2e9895420bcb5392c9333db8bab4</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1768781109</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="School Site">
      <data key="d0">School Site</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">A School Site is a location designated for educational purposes and owned by the entity responsible for running a school.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a7aa2e9895420bcb5392c9333db8bab4</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1768781109</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Roadway">
      <data key="d0">Roadway</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Roadway refers to the infrastructure that allows for vehicle travel as governed by Planning and Zoning ordinances.&lt;SEP&gt;Roadway refers to the portion of a right-of-way that is improved for motor vehicle travel, including vehicle travel lanes and on-street parking areas.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a7aa2e9895420bcb5392c9333db8bab4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-028c3c84ee58774048f304cfbc47c40a</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1768781109</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Scenic View Corridor">
      <data key="d0">Scenic View Corridor</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Scenic View Corridor is an area that may include scenic views along it and values for its intrinsic scenic qualities.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a7aa2e9895420bcb5392c9333db8bab4</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1768781109</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Helicopter Landing Facilities">
      <data key="d0">Helicopter Landing Facilities</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Helicopter Landing Facilities (HLFs) are designated areas specifically planned and regulated for the landing and takeoff of helicopters. These facilities play a crucial role in urban and rural planning, integrating with existing infrastructure to provide efficient access for rotorcraft operations. HLFs are essential for ensuring both safety and public interest, as they are evaluated based on various safety criteria and their compatibility with surrounding land uses.

Governed by specific regulatory requirements, such as those outlined in Chapter 33.243, HLFs must adhere to stringent guidelines to mitigate risks and promote operational safety. The regulatory framework not only ensures the safety of helicopter operations but also addresses the unique land use impacts and benefits these facilities may have on their environments.

In summary, Helicopter Landing Facilities are vital components of aviation infrastructure, essential for facilitating rotorcraft operations while aligning with safety standards and community interests. Their implementation requires thorough planning and ongoing compliance with regulatory criteria to maintain the safety and efficiency of helicopter landings and takeoffs.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-dd5e24f7c98b2b609a3ed6656b608420&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-17a8112955d64121a20078f6914b8018&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-21fff4b96769197d29b5065fb50894e2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-916bbd63b5bfa86ff2fa9073870d300d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-31ca5fc3d28aa819abc484b86dc468ed&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a6144b313ccb84dbccd73dbf4dd1e5c6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-48a997e1b200b6816ddf145fba6c1929&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2215c0dede8ee98027b1212757bd97af</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1768781109</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Front Setback">
      <data key="d0">Front Setback</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Front Setback is the minimum distance required between a specified object and the front lot line.&lt;SEP&gt;Front setback is measured from a front lot line.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a7aa2e9895420bcb5392c9333db8bab4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1ad3df252c10f7d3d9869375a3dd70b5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-606354f765e28b95d29b753fc9fe43fd</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1768781109</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Rear Setback">
      <data key="d0">Rear Setback</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Rear Setback is the minimum distance required between a specified object and the rear lot line.&lt;SEP&gt;Rear setback is measured from a rear lot line.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a7aa2e9895420bcb5392c9333db8bab4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1ad3df252c10f7d3d9869375a3dd70b5</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1768781109</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Subdivisions">
      <data key="d0">Subdivisions</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Subdivisions refer to divisions of land for development that are subject to specific planning and zoning regulations.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c2e596f70f1bcab1802ff190bf7807a1</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1768781109</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="PUDs">
      <data key="d0">PUDs</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">PUDs, or Planned Unit Developments, are a type of development that includes mixed-use areas and typically requires special review.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c2e596f70f1bcab1802ff190bf7807a1</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1768781109</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="ground floor window standard">
      <data key="d0">ground floor window standard</data>
