    <node id="Existing Paths">
      <data key="d0">Existing Paths</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Existing paths refer to the pathways that may be either new or already established and must not conflict with certain conservation criteria.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3f72437166c353de768d234823279f24</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1768781114</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Sign Projects">
      <data key="d0">Sign Projects</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Sign projects refer to the requirements and dimensions for new and existing signs as specified in the rules.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3f72437166c353de768d234823279f24</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1768781114</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Natural Resources Management Plan">
      <data key="d0">Natural Resources Management Plan</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">The Natural Resources Management Plan provides guidelines that the PIR Master Plan must follow, particularly for the Peninsula Drainage District No. 1.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8c25a4b5dcdfc23b3bd64f8c6d4bbc00</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1768781114</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Subdistricts">
      <data key="d0">Subdistricts</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Subdistricts refer to delineated areas within the plan district with specific characteristics and requirements outlined in the PIR Master Plan.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8c25a4b5dcdfc23b3bd64f8c6d4bbc00&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e038f4d3687c0baca987d663a82852cc</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1768781114</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Traffic Management Plan">
      <data key="d0">Traffic Management Plan</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">The Traffic Management Plan is a proposed strategy addressing the traffic impacts and mitigation measures associated with the PIR Master Plan.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8c25a4b5dcdfc23b3bd64f8c6d4bbc00</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1768781114</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Chapter 33.566">
      <data key="d0">Chapter 33.566</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Chapter 33.566 outlines the criteria for the approval of the PIR Master Plan.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8c25a4b5dcdfc23b3bd64f8c6d4bbc00</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1768781114</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Land Division Regulations">
      <data key="d0">Land Division Regulations</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Land Division Regulations refer to the rules governing residential zoning and density standards for land development.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-09653fd8cb264cda4f3441bd478fe017</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1768781114</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Residential Zones">
      <data key="d0">Residential Zones</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Residential zones refer to areas where the regulations apply to residential uses and the alteration of structures.&lt;SEP&gt;Residential Zones are areas designated for residential use where development regulations for Helicopter Landing Facilities are stricter.&lt;SEP&gt;Residential Zones refer to areas designated for housing that have specific regulations regarding building height and density to maintain community integrity.&lt;SEP&gt;Residential Zones refer to areas designated for residential development and are mentioned in relation to building regulations.&lt;SEP&gt;Residential Zones are designated areas intended for housing development and are subject to specific zoning regulations.&lt;SEP&gt;Residential Zones are areas designated for residential living that are impacted by the approval criteria for conditional uses.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-09653fd8cb264cda4f3441bd478fe017&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-21fff4b96769197d29b5065fb50894e2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f3994dbc2c6f464ef189000d5ce6654c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-48080692bc899a8f3606cd61bb319bbb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c7643c0f4d3b8e865b2421a8cf1882d9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f4e7feaddec557941c697fec7b2e2763&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-552034cbee7413e9d44334c40a7cb832</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1768781114</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Recreation Area Standards">
      <data key="d0">Recreation Area Standards</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Recreation Area Standards outline the requirements for designated recreational spaces within land divisions, including size and ownership.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-09653fd8cb264cda4f3441bd478fe017</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1768781114</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Construction Timing Agreement">
      <data key="d0">Construction Timing Agreement</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">A Construction Timing Agreement stipulates the schedule and timeline for the installation of improvements in land divisions.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-09653fd8cb264cda4f3441bd478fe017</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1768781114</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="RF-RM1 Zone">
      <data key="d0">RF-RM1 Zone</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The RF-RM1 Zone is a residential zoning designation that specifies requirements for recreation area tracts within land divisions.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-09653fd8cb264cda4f3441bd478fe017</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1768781114</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="RM2-RX Zone">
      <data key="d0">RM2-RX Zone</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The RM2-RX Zone is a residential zone with particular standards concerning recreation area development and requirements.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-09653fd8cb264cda4f3441bd478fe017</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1768781114</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Subsection B">
      <data key="d0">Subsection B</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Subsection B details the prohibited uses related to accessory home occupations, including specific types of repairs and assembly of vehicles.&lt;SEP&gt;Subsection B also defines standards applicable when the light rail line is adjacent to a lot line.&lt;SEP&gt;Subsection B outlines the procedures for proposals that affect the development or reduce the conditional use site boundary.&lt;SEP&gt;Subsection B discusses how developments with multiple primary uses are classified within use categories.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-930256b3bb4008d11b0886f29973ea67&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6d3f9ffb1d8c7f041a89cac2f7df9d56&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-96815c5a54ec29fd3986869ba3cf59e6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a89d07e7c36f6a7d1b3f9ac2e0c0baf5</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1768781114</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Examples Subsection">
      <data key="d0">Examples Subsection</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The Examples Subsection provides instances of categories and how specific uses are classified under those categories.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-930256b3bb4008d11b0886f29973ea67</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1768781114</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Cross-Levee Trail">
      <data key="d0">Cross-Levee Trail</data>
      <data key="d1">location</data>
      <data key="d2">The Cross-Levee Trail is an area that must adhere to specific zoning regulations as part of the Columbia South Shore Slough Trail area.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-4749d2209607255edbe985025b727249</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1768781114</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Major Public Trail Symbol">
      <data key="d0">Major Public Trail Symbol</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The Major Public Trail Symbol indicates lands designated for constructing major public trails on the zoning map.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-4749d2209607255edbe985025b727249</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1768781114</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Certificate of Occupancy">
      <data key="d0">Certificate of Occupancy</data>
      <data key="d1">document</data>
      <data key="d2">A Certificate of Occupancy is an official document confirming that a building is compliant with applicable building codes and is safe for occupancy.&lt;SEP&gt;A Certificate of Occupancy is a document that certifies that a building complies with building codes and zoning laws, allowing it to be occupied.&lt;SEP&gt;Certificate of Occupancy is a document issued to signify that a building is compliant with local codes and is ready for use.&lt;SEP&gt;A certificate of occupancy is issued by the Planning and Development department upon the completion of a building permit or occupancy change.&lt;SEP&gt;A Certificate of Occupancy is a document issued confirming completion of a building permit or change of occupancy.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-4749d2209607255edbe985025b727249&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-11738f034d5f65fb344c5bf37019ca51&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-68c858bb23efe67cb70612c0464f3e80&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b2d0e3bbe363516aa161846014338967&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3a2edce75cc1c84e8d78d82be31fd43b</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1768781114</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Permitted Original Art Mural">
      <data key="d0">Permitted Original Art Mural</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Permitted Original Art Mural is a type of artwork defined by local ordinances for display on buildings.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-79f19e6435928fe4a325e8a1a4cc5dd1</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1768781114</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Video or Digital Cameras">
      <data key="d0">Video or Digital Cameras</data>
      <data key="d1">artifact</data>
      <data key="d2">Video or Digital Cameras are devices used for surveillance, described in the context of building regulation compliance.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-79f19e6435928fe4a325e8a1a4cc5dd1</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1768781114</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Roof Alterations Regulations">
      <data key="d0">Roof Alterations Regulations</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Roof Alterations Regulations are guidelines governing the modifications and additions allowed on buildings' roofs based on specific parameters.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-79f19e6435928fe4a325e8a1a4cc5dd1</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1768781114</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Public Street and Sidewalk Improvements">
      <data key="d0">Public Street and Sidewalk Improvements</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Public street and sidewalk improvements refer to urban development activities that do not expand existing disturbance areas.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-dc38fc86828ecd90586e33007486e5d4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-70d37b0db2824c732a3a9a02ff910d85</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1768781114</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Paved Parking Lots">
      <data key="d0">Paved Parking Lots</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Paved parking lots are constructed in transition areas as part of development plans, allowing for improved vehicle access and management.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-dc38fc86828ecd90586e33007486e5d4</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1768781114</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Archaeological Resource Recovery Plan">
      <data key="d0">Archaeological Resource Recovery Plan</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">An archaeological resource recovery plan outlines the process for removing archaeological materials while ensuring compliance with regulations and tribal consultations.&lt;SEP&gt;The Archaeological Resource Recovery Plan outlines requirements for documenting compliance with resource recovery activities as per regulations.&lt;SEP&gt;The archaeological resource recovery plan details strategies for managing and preserving archaeological resources during development.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-dc38fc86828ecd90586e33007486e5d4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-903c333e61f6db5523241ff9b270a90f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-df192280c989d3e1d2bc59bf520124ed</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1768781114</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Tribes">
      <data key="d0">Tribes</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Tribes represent tribal governments that participate in consultations regarding archaeological surveys and resource recovery plans to protect cultural heritage.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-dc38fc86828ecd90586e33007486e5d4</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1768781114</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Memorandum of Understanding (MOU)">
      <data key="d0">Memorandum of Understanding (MOU)</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">A Memorandum of Understanding (MOU) is developed between parties to outline agreements regarding the care and disposition of archaeological materials.&lt;SEP&gt;The MOU is a formal agreement that outlines the care, communication, and responsibilities regarding archaeological resources between involved parties.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-dc38fc86828ecd90586e33007486e5d4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-df192280c989d3e1d2bc59bf520124ed</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1768781114</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Archaeological Evaluation">
      <data key="d0">Archaeological Evaluation</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">An archaeological evaluation is a detailed assessment conducted by a qualified archaeologist to determine the presence of archaeological materials before development activities.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-dc38fc86828ecd90586e33007486e5d4</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1768781114</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Ground Disturbing Activities">
      <data key="d0">Ground Disturbing Activities</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Ground disturbing activities refer to actions that disturb the soil and can affect archaeological resources, subject to regulations.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-dc38fc86828ecd90586e33007486e5d4</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1768781114</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Building Footprint">
      <data key="d0">Building Footprint</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Building footprint is the perimeter of a building established by the exterior faces of the foundation, excluding certain parts like porches and decks.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-dc38fc86828ecd90586e33007486e5d4</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1768781114</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Chapter 33.605 through 33.612">
      <data key="d0">Chapter 33.605 through 33.612</data>
      <data key="d1">document</data>
      <data key="d2">Chapters 33.605 through 33.612 contain regulations related to land divisions that must be satisfied for approval.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f464bb6caf4196f9f098e5debb9f7fc4</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1768781114</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Chapter 33.673.200">
      <data key="d0">Chapter 33.673.200</data>
      <data key="d1">document</data>
      <data key="d2">Chapter 33.673.200 contains regulations regarding specific changes to approved Preliminary Plans that may be considered under certain conditions.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f464bb6caf4196f9f098e5debb9f7fc4</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1768781114</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Commercial Parking Facilities">
      <data key="d0">Commercial Parking Facilities</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Commercial parking facilities are structures meant to provide parking but must conform to specific criteria to support local development without negatively impacting the area's character.&lt;SEP&gt;Commercial Parking Facilities refer to designated areas that provide parking solutions for various commercial uses in urban development.&lt;SEP&gt;Commercial parking facilities are designated areas for parking vehicles that must meet certain standards and regulations.&lt;SEP&gt;Commercial Parking Facilities refer to parking spaces that are available for public use or rent, but are not considered accessory to a primary use.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a901459905390cbd8ce9c02b74a9fd1f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6798d89accc1ff81b347ffdd52d0e6c7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-321a1a12fd260a04f2fbd3343d904e35&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-44cf0420ca13ed6c3eed49130f77fb31&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-441c51a51f44fd3d962a1948c121fd0f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-71f465b3d8f13fedcc409a730049f471</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1768781114</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Accessory Parking Facilities">
      <data key="d0">Accessory Parking Facilities</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Accessory Parking Facilities are parking spaces that are associated with a primary use, not classified as Commercial Parking facilities, even if leasing is involved.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a901459905390cbd8ce9c02b74a9fd1f</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1768781114</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Public Transit Park-and-Ride Facilities">
      <data key="d0">Public Transit Park-and-Ride Facilities</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Public Transit Park-and-Ride Facilities provide parking spaces for individuals to use public transit but are classified as Community Services.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a901459905390cbd8ce9c02b74a9fd1f</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1768781114</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Full-Serve Gas Stations">
      <data key="d0">Full-Serve Gas Stations</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Full-Serve Gas Stations are gas stations where attendants provide service to customers, classified under Quick Vehicle Servicing.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a901459905390cbd8ce9c02b74a9fd1f</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1768781114</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Mini-Serve Gas Stations">
      <data key="d0">Mini-Serve Gas Stations</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Mini-Serve Gas Stations are gas stations where limited service is provided, also classified under Quick Vehicle Servicing.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a901459905390cbd8ce9c02b74a9fd1f</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1768781114</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Accessory Uses in Major Event Entertainment">
      <data key="d0">Accessory Uses in Major Event Entertainment</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Accessory Uses in Major Event Entertainment are additional services that support major events, such as restaurants and parking.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a901459905390cbd8ce9c02b74a9fd1f</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1768781114</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Traditional Office">
      <data key="d0">Traditional Office</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Traditional Office refers to a category of use in zoning regulations allowing conventional office spaces and functions.&lt;SEP&gt;Traditional Office focuses on businesses in areas like government, medical, and financial services, requiring regular client visits.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a901459905390cbd8ce9c02b74a9fd1f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d08b01451771b358c77494f198e0c0a4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-157f892dc3db1b7d5113b58ec80ccd43</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1768781114</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Industrial Office">
      <data key="d0">Industrial Office</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Industrial Office refers to a category of use in zoning regulations allowing office environments related to industrial activities.&lt;SEP&gt;Industrial Office is a classification of building use that can occupy up to 60,000 square feet as per zoning regulations, allowing for specific office functionalities within industrial spaces.&lt;SEP&gt;Industrial Office emphasizes science and technology services, primarily catering to other businesses without frequent client visits.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a901459905390cbd8ce9c02b74a9fd1f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d08b01451771b358c77494f198e0c0a4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-acfa11b893b09fc1fb7d6655be073aa3</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1768781114</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Examples of Traditional Office">
      <data key="d0">Examples of Traditional Office</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Examples include various professional services such as legal, accounting, and real estate services, as well as public utility offices.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a901459905390cbd8ce9c02b74a9fd1f</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1768781114</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Examples of Industrial Office">
      <data key="d0">Examples of Industrial Office</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Examples include engineering services, design services, and various labs primarily focused on testing for practitioners.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a901459905390cbd8ce9c02b74a9fd1f</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1768781114</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Fleet Vehicles">
      <data key="d0">Fleet Vehicles</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Fleet Vehicles refer to vehicles that are owned and operated by a specific organization for business purposes.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a901459905390cbd8ce9c02b74a9fd1f</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1768781114</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Truck Stops">
      <data key="d0">Truck Stops</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
