    <node id="Ground Floor Windows">
      <data key="d0">Ground Floor Windows</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Ground Floor Windows are architectural features of buildings that specifically refer to the window installations located on street-facing elevations. These windows are subjected to regulations that stipulate they must cover a certain percentage of the facade area, thereby enhancing both pedestrian experiences and safety. 

Ground Floor Windows are particularly significant for residential units, as their design and placement are aimed at improving visibility and fostering interaction with the surrounding streetscape. The standards governing these windows encompass not just the number but also the design aspects, ensuring that they create a welcoming environment for pedestrians while complying with specific architectural guidelines.

Furthermore, Ground Floor Windows can include provisions for alternatives such as public art, which adds cultural value and aesthetic interest to the building façade. Overall, these windows serve as crucial openings that facilitate a visual and physical connection between the interiors of buildings and the public spaces outside, thus enhancing engagement at the pedestrian level and contributing positively to urban design.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-eebc2b12ebf015db39e2792d62d9565e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a2020f8cb9e1e9fff0f2699c2142d64b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d37ff07ffa7013303eef3ff22ca4c664&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b45e62181c08d6aef102ffa4458a5226&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2574af8787db7883bc15da7cbabd3a3b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4ab8e6f4ae4a675657fc86e7ca06a385&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9edcce0b9a08e3510e6bed7afd6cc6a2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a7282e754cd453819f3e67c09c8e7a91</data>
